\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 The first census of the Israelites by Moses
\p
\v 1 In April of the year after the Israelite people escaped from Egypt, Yahweh spoke to Moses in the Sinai wilderness in the sacred tent, saying,
\v 2 “Count all the men who are Israel’s descendants by their clans and families, being sure to list all their names.
\v 3 \add Only include those\add* who are twenty or older and able \add to be recruited\add* as soldiers. You and Aaron must enrol them into military divisions.
\v 4 The leader of each tribe will accompany you.
\p
\v 5 These are the names of the men who will assist you:
\li1 Elitsur (Shedeyur’s son) from Reuben,
\li1
\v 6 Shelumiel (Tsurishadai’s son) from Simeon,
\li1
\v 7 Nahshon (Amminadab’s son) from Judah,
\li1
\v 8 Netanel (Tsuar’s son) from Issachar,
\li1
\v 9 Eliav (Helon’s son) from Zebulun,
\li1
\v 10 Elishama (Amihud’s son) from Joseph’s son Ephraim,
\li1 Gamaliel (Pedahtsur’s son) from Joseph’s son Manasseh,
\li1
\v 11 Avidan (Gid’oni’s son) from Benjamin,
\li1
\v 12 Ahiezer (Amishaday’s son) from Dan,
\li1
\v 13 Pagiel (Okran’s son) from Asher,
\li1
\v 14 Elyasaf (Re’uel’s son) from Gad,
\li1
\v 15 Ahira (Eynan’s son) from Naphtali.
\v 16 They were the leaders of their tribes—chosen out of their communities, and leaders of \add units of\add* one thousand \add soldiers\add*.
\p
\v 17 Moses and Aaron took those men whose names had been given
\v 18 and they assembled the entire community that April. For each tribe, they recorded their ancestry by clan, counting the names of those men who were twenty or over,
\v 19 just as Yahweh had commanded Moses. So he counted them \add there\add* in the Sinai wilderness,
\v 20-21 individually counting those \add in each tribe below\add* by their clan, and only including males who were twenty or older and able to fight in battle:
\li1 46,500 men from the Reuben tribe,
\li1
\v 22-23 59,300 men from the Simeon tribe,
\li1
\v 24-25 45,650 men from the Gad tribe,
\li1
\v 26-27 74,600 men from the Judah tribe,
\li1
\v 28-29 54,400 men from the Issachar tribe,
\li1
\v 30-31 57,400 men from the Zebulun tribe,
\li1
\v 32-33 40,500 men from the Ephraim tribe,
\li1
\v 34-35 32,200 men from the Manasseh tribe,
\li1
\v 36-37 35,400 men from the Benjamin tribe,
\li1
\v 38-39 62,700 men from the Dan tribe,
\li1
\v 40-41 41,500 men from the Asher tribe,
\li1
\v 42-43 53,400 men from the Naphtali tribe.
\p
\v 44 Those were the ones that Moses and Aaron and the twelve Israelite leaders (one from each tribe) listed.
\v 45 They were all the Israelites who were counted according to their ancestry, being men who were twenty or older, and capable of going off to battle—
\v 46 603,550 of them in total.
\v 47 However, the Levites weren’t listed along with the other tribes
\v 48 because Yahweh had told Moses,
\v 49 “Don’t list the Levites or count them with the other Israelites.
\v 50 Instead, appoint them to manage the tent where the copy of the agreement is kept, along with all its equipment. They must serve inside it as \add required\add*, and they should set their tents up around it.
\v 51 When it’s time to leave that place, the Levites must take the tent down and when camp is set up, they must erect it \add again\add*, but any stranger who approaches it must be executed.
\v 52 \add When\add* the \add other\add* Israelites set up camp, each man must set up with his army division by their banner,
\v 53 but the Levites will camp there around the sacred tent and look after it. That way, the Israelite community won’t make \add Yahweh\add* angry.
\v 54 So the Israelites did everything that Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\c 2
\s1 Camp layout by tribe
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron:
\p
\v 2 “The Israelites must camp with each family joining their tribe, which will be displaying its banner. They must camp around the sacred tent but at a distance.
\p
\v 3 On the eastern side facing the sunrise will be Judah’s banner and their army divisions, led by Amminadab’s son Nahshon
\v 4 with 74,600 in his division.
\v 5 The Issachar tribe will set up next to them, led by Tsuar’s son Netanel,
\v 6 with 54,400 in his division.
\v 7 Then the Zebulun tribe, led by Helon’s son Eliav,
\v 8 with 57,400 in his division.
\v 9 The total number in the divisions in the Judah camp is 186,400, and when travelling, they’ll all go at the front.
\p
\v 10 On the southern side \add of the sacred tent\add* will be Reuben’s banner, led by Shedeyur’s son Elitsur
\v 11 with 46,500 in his division.
\v 12 The Simeon tribe will set up next to them, led by Tsurishadai’s son Shelumiel
\v 13 with 59,300 in his division.
\v 14 Then the Gad tribe, led by Re’uel’s son Elyasaf.
\v 15 with 45,650 in his division.
\v 16 The total number in the divisions in the Reuben camp is 151,450, and when travelling, they’ll all go second.
\p
\v 17 Then the Levites will go next with their own banners in the order that they were camped, \add carrying\add* the sacred tent.
\p
\v 18 On the western side \add of the sacred tent\add* will be Ephraim’s banner, led by Amihud’s son Elishama
\v 19 with 40,500 in his division.
\v 20 The Manasseh tribe will set up next to them, led by Pedahtsur’s son Gamaliel
\v 21 with 32,200 in his division.
\v 22 Then the Benjamin tribe led by Gid’oni’s son Avidan
\v 23 with 35,400 in his division.
\v 24 The total number in the divisions in the Ephraim camp is 108,100, and when travelling, they’ll all go third.
\p
\v 25 On the northern side \add of the sacred tent\add* will be Dan’s banner, led by Amishaday’s son Ahiezer
\v 26 with 62,700 in his division.
\v 27 The Asher tribe will set up next to them, led by Okran’s son Pagiel
\v 28 with 41,500 in his division.
\v 29 Then the Naphtali tribe led by Eynan’s son Ahira
\v 30 with 53,400 in his division.
\v 31 The total number in the divisions in the Dan camp is 157,600, and when travelling, they’ll all go last with their banners.
\p
\v 32 Those are the listed Israelites by their ancestral tribes with a total of 603,500 fighting men.
\v 33 However, following Yahweh’s instructions, the Levites weren’t listed among them.
\p
\v 34 So the Israelites did everything that Yahweh commanded Moses. They camped under their banners, and travelled grouped in their tribes and clans.
\c 3
\s1 Aaron’s sons
\p
\v 1 The following are the descendants of Aaron and Moses as on the day when Yahweh spoke with Moses on Mt. Sinai:
\v 2 Aaron’s sons were: Nadav (the oldest), Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar.
\v 3 They were \add all\add* anointed as priests and served in that role,
\v 4 except that Nadav and Avihu died in front of Yahweh when they offered unacceptable fire to him in the Sinai wilderness, and \add so\add* they had no sons. So Eleazar and Itamar served as priests alongside their father Aaron.
\s1 The Levites as ransom for their sons
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 6 “Summon the Levites to come and stand in front of Aaron the priest, so they can assist him.
\v 7 They must perform duties for him and for the entire community at and around the sacred tent,
\v 8 including looking after all of its equipment and fulfilling the obligations of the Israelites.
\v 9 You must assign the Levites to Aaron—out of all the Israelites it’s them who are assigned \add to serve the priests\add*.
\v 10 Appoint Aaron and his sons as priests and to preserve the priesthood. Any other person who tries to act as a priest must be executed.”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 12 “Out of all the Israelites, I’ve taken the Levites instead of the oldest son from every family. The Levites will be the ransom for those sons, and so they belong to me,
\v 13 because every firstborn male person and animal is set aside as belonging to me ever since I killed those firstborn males in Egypt. I am Yahweh.”
\s1 The census of the Levites
\p
\v 14 Then in the Sinai wilderness, Yahweh told Moses,
\v 15 “List all the male Levites that are at least one month old. Count them by their families and clans.”
\v 16 So Moses listed them exactly as Yahweh had commanded him to,
\v 17 and these were Levi’s sons: Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\v 18 Gereshon’s sons were: Livni and Shimei.
\v 19 Kohat’s sons were: Amram, Yitshar, Hebron, and Uziel.
\v 20 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi.
\m Those were the families of the Levites by their clans.
\p
\v 21 Gereshon’s descendants were the Livnim and the Shim’im, so they made up the Gershonim.
\v 22 They had 7,500 males that were at least a month old.
\v 23 The Gershoni clans were to camp on the western side of the sacred tent,
\v 24 and their leader was La’el’s son Elyasaf.
\v 25 Gereshon’s descendants were responsible for the sacred tent, including its covering and the entrance curtain,
\v 26 the courtyard curtains, and the courtyard entrance curtain, that surrounded it. Also the ropes used to hold them.
\p
\v 27 Kohat’s descendants were the Amramim, the Yitshari, the Hevronim, and the Uzielim, so they made up the Kohatites.
\v 28 They had 8,600 males that were at least a month old. They were in charge of the running of the sacred tent.
\v 29 The Kohatite clans were to camp of the southern side of the sacred tent,
\v 30 and their leader was Uziel’s son Elitsafan.
\v 31 They were in charge of the box, the table and the lampstand, the altars and all the utensils used for ministry, the \add inside\add* curtain and its gear.
\p
\v 32 The man who organised the Levites was Aaron’s son, Eleazar the priest. He had oversight over all those who were responsible for the sacred tent.
\p
\v 33 Merari’s descendants were the Mahlim and the Mushim, so they made up the Merarim.
\v 34 They had 6,200 males that were at least a month old,
\v 35 and their leader was Avihayil’s son Tsuriel. They were to camp on the northern side of the sacred tent.
\v 36 They were in charge of all the frames for the sacred tent, its crossbars, posts, bases, all its containers and equipment,
\v 37 as well as the pillars \add used to make\add* the courtyard with their bases, pegs, and ropes.
\p
\v 38 Moses and Aaron and his sons set up their tents in front of \add the entrance on\add* the eastern side of the sacred tent. They were in charge of that tent and the needs of the Israelites. Any stranger who approached had to be executed.
\v 39 Moses and Aaron listed 22,000 Levite males that were at least a month old, listing them by their clans as per Yahweh’s instructions.
\s1 Paying the price for the firstborn sons
\p
\v 40 Then Yahweh told Moses, “List all the \add other\add* male Israelite that are at least one month old and count them.
\v 41 The Levites are for me, Yahweh, in the place of the firstborn males from every Israelite family, and their animals are in place of the Israelites’ firstborn male animals.”
\v 42 So Moses listed the firstborn Israelites exactly as Yahweh had commanded him to.
\v 43 and the total number of firstborn males a month old or more whose names were listed came to 22,273.
\p
\v 44 Then Yahweh spoke to Moses,
\v 45 “Take the Levites instead of every firstborn male Israelite, and the Levites’ animals instead of their animals, and the Levites will be mine—I am Yahweh.
\v 46 To redeem the 273 firstborn Israelites who outnumber the Levites,
\v 47 collect five silver coins for each one of them. (Use the same silver coins weighing eleven grams, that the priests use in the sacred tent.)
\v 48 Give that money to Aaron and his sons as payment of the ransom for those excess 273 males.”
\v 49 So Moses collected that ransom money to cover the ransom of the excess \add not covered by the number of\add* Levites.
\v 50 He collected 1,365 silver coins from the firstborn Israelite males (using the standard weight of silver from the sanctuary)
\v 51 and gave it to Aaron and his sons just as Yahweh had commanded.
\c 4
\s1 Kohat’s descendants’ duties
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron,
\v 2 “Take a detailed census of Kohat’s male descendants by their clans and families
\v 3 who are from twenty-five up to fifty years old and who goes into the sacred tent to minister.
\p
\v 4 This is what the Kohatites are responsible for concerning the sacred tent including the inner room:
\v 5 When it’s time to break camp, Aaron and his sons must enter and take down the curtain that veils it, and then use that to cover the box containing the agreement.
\v 6 Then they must cover it with fine leather, followed by a blue cloth, and then put its \add carrying\add* poles \add through the rings\add*.
\p
\v 7 They must spread a blue cloth over the sacred bread table, then place the dishes, pans, bowls, and jugs for the drink offerings on top of that, along with the sacred bread.
\v 8 Then they must spread a scarlet cloth over all that, and cover that again with fine leather, and then put its poles \add through the rings\add*.
\p
\v 9 They must spread a blue cloth over the lampstand with its \add oil\add* lamps, its tongs and trays, and the oil containers that are used for it.
\v 10 Then they must wrap the lampstand and all its equipment in fine leather, and place it \add all\add* on its carrying frame.
\p
\v 11 Then the must spread a blue cloth over the gold \add incense\add* altar, and cover that again with fine leather, and then put its poles \add through the rings\add*.
\v 12 Then they must get all the \add other\add* utensils that they use in the sacred tent, and cover that again with fine leather, and place it \add all\add* on its carrying frame.
\v 13 They need to remove the ashes from the \add bronze\add* altar and spread a purple cloth over it,
\v 14 and put all the utensils they use on top of it: the trays, meat forks, shovel and basins for the altar. Then they must cover it with fine leather, and then put its poles \add through the rings\add*.
\v 15 Aaron and his sons must finish covering the sacred tent and all its utensils and equipment when they’re about to move camp. Then the Kohatites can come to carry it all without touching the inner room so they won’t die. That’s the responsibility of Kohat’s descendants—\add carrying\add* the sacred tent.
\p
\v 16 Eleazar (son of Aaron the priest) is in charge of the lamp oil and the fragrant incense, the regular grain offering, the anointing oil, and the oversight of the sacred tent and the inner room, including everything that’s used inside them.
\p
\v 17 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron,
\v 18 “Don’t allow the Kohatite Levites to get wiped out \add by mishandling the most sacred items\add*,
\v 19 but take these precautions on their behalf so they won’t die when they approach the most sacred items: Get Aaron and his sons to go and show each of them personally the correct and proper way to do his duties.
\v 20 However, they must never \add go early\add* to set their eyes on the sacred objects, even briefly, or they’ll die.”
\s1 Gereshon’s descendants’ duties
\p
\v 21 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 22 “Take a detailed census of Gereshon’s male descendants by their clans and families
\v 23 who are from thirty up to fifty years old and who goes into the sacred tent to minister.
\p
\v 24 This is what the Gershonim are responsible for working with and carrying:
\v 25 They must carry the \add inner\add* curtains from the sacred tent, the covering, the fine leather covering, the entrance curtain,
\v 26 the courtyard curtains, and the courtyard gate curtain, along with the ropes and other equipment for using them. That will be their ministry.
\v 27 Aaron and his sons will direct the Gershonim in their duties and assign all \add the items\add* to them.
\v 28 That will be the ministry of Gereshon’s descendants with the sacred tent, and it will all be under the supervision of Aaron’s son Itamar the priest.
\s1 Merari’s descendants’ duties
\p
\v 29 “Take a detailed census of Merari’s descendants by their clans and families,
\v 30 listing the men who are from thirty to fifty years old and who goes into the sacred tent to minister.
\v 31 Their duties are carrying the frames, crossbars, posts, and sockets for the sacred tent,
\v 32 the courtyard pillars and their bases, their pegs and ropes, and all their equipment. Every man must be assigned by name as to what equipment he must carry.
\v 33 That will be the ministry of Merari’s descendants with the sacred tent, and it will all be under the supervision of Aaron’s son Itamar the priest.
\s1 The totals from the Levite census
\p
\v 34 So Moses and Aaron and the community leaders listed the Kohatites by their clans and families,
\v 35 \add counting\add* the men from thirty to fifty years old who go into the sacred tent to minister,
\v 36 and the total for all the clans was 2,750.
\v 37 That was from the Kohatite clans who ministered at the sacred tent and were listed by Moses and Aaron just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 38 They counted the Gershonim by their clans and families,
\v 39 \add counting\add* the men from thirty to fifty years old who go into the sacred tent to minister,
\v 40 and the total for all the clans was 2,630.
\v 41 That was from the Gershoni clans who ministered at the sacred tent and were listed by Moses and Aaron just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 42 They counted the Merarim by their clans and families,
\v 43 \add counting\add* the men from thirty to fifty years old who go into the sacred tent to minister,
\v 44 and the total for all the clans was 3,200.
\v 45 That was from the Merari clans who ministered at the sacred tent and were listed by Moses and Aaron just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 46 So Moses, Aaron, and the Israelite leaders counted \add all\add* the Levites by their clans and families,
\v 47 \add counting\add* the men from thirty to fifty years old who go into the sacred tent to minister and also help with carrying it,
\v 48 and the total came to 8,580.
\v 49 Those were the ones counted by Moses following Yahweh’s instructions. Each man’s name was listed by Moses, along with his duties and what was his responsibility to carry.
\c 5
\s1 Handling ceremonial ‘uncleanness’
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Order the Israelites that anyone with a skin disease or a discharge, and anyone who’s ‘unclean’ because they touched a corpse, must be sent outside the camp.
\v 3 Whether they’re male or female, they must be sent outside the camp so that the whole camp doesn’t become ‘unclean’ \add because\add* I’m living there \add right\add* amongst them.”
\v 4 So the Israelites did what Yahweh told Moses, and sent those people outside the camp.
\s1 Reparations for crimes
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 6 “Tell the Israelites that when any man or a woman disobeys God by doing any of the things that humans do when they’re unfaithful towards Yahweh, then that person is guilty
\v 7 and they must confess their disobedience. Then that person must make full reparation to whoever they wronged, along with an extra twenty percent \add of the cost\add*.
\v 8 However, if reparation to the victim isn’t possible \add for some reason\add*, then it must be paid to Yahweh via a priest, in addition to the ram \add that will be offered\add* for that person’s atonement.
\v 9 Any sacred contributions by the Israelites that are presented to a priest will belong to him.
\v 10 Each person’s sacred contributions will be their own, \add but\add* anything given to a priest will belong to him.”
\s1 Testing for an unfaithful wife
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 12 “Tell the Israelites that if any man’s wife has an affair and is unfaithful towards him,
\v 13 when another man secretly has sex with her, then she becomes ‘unclean’, even though there’s no witness against her and she wasn’t caught.
\v 14 If the husband becomes suspicious, whether or not she’s actually become ‘unclean’,
\v 15 then he should take her to a priest. He must also bring an offering on her behalf: 1.6 kilograms of barley flour. It must not have any oil or spices mixed into it because it’s a grain offering for suspicion—for determining if unfaithfulness has occurred.
\p
\v 16 Then the priest must lead her forward and stand her in front of Yahweh.
\v 17 He must fill a pottery jar with sacred water, and then mix some dust from the floor of Yahweh’s residence into the water.
\v 18 Then the priest must stand the woman in front of Yahweh and let her hair down, then he must place the suspicion grain offering on her palm. (It’s a grain offering for jealousy.) The priest must be holding the water of bitterness that brings a curse
\v 19 and tell the woman to make this oath: “If no \add other\add* man slept with you, and if you didn’t dirty yourself while you’re married, then be free from this water of bitterness that brings a curse.
\v 20 But if you have cheated on your husband and become ‘unclean’ and slept with some other guy, \add there’ll be consequences\add*.
\v 21 Then the priest will make the woman take that curse as an oath, and he’ll tell her, “Yahweh will make your experience a curse and your people will see the results of the oath, when Yahweh gives disease to your genitals and makes your belly swell.
\v 22 This water will invoke the curse to make your belly swell and your genitals shrivel.” Then the woman must say, “Indeed, let it be so.”
\p
\v 23 Then the priest must write those curses on a scroll, then wipe \add the ink\add* into the water of bitterness.
\v 24 He must make the woman drink that water of bitterness that brings the curse, and it will enter into her.
\v 25 Then he’ll take the grain offering of suspicion from her hand, and hold it up in front of Yahweh, and then take it to the altar.
\v 26 He’ll take some of the grain as a memorial portion and burn it on the altar. Then he’ll make the woman drink the water.
\v 27 After she’s drunk it, if she was ‘unclean’ and unfaithful to her husband, the water will carry that curse into her, and her belly will swell and her genitals will get diseased, and the woman will become a curse among her people.
\v 28 However, if she hadn’t defiled herself and is pure, then she’ll be free and \add be able to\add* have children.
\p
\v 29 That’s the rule about jealousy when a wife cheats on her husband and defiles herself,
\v 30 or when a husband becomes jealous and suspicious and he stands his wife in front of Yahweh, and the priest applies this ruling to her.
\v 31 Then the husband will be innocent, and that women will bear the consequences of her wrongdoing.
\c 6
\s1 Instructions for the Nazirite vow
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that when any man or woman wants to make a solemn vow to separate themself to Yahweh as a Nazirite,
\v 3 they must keep away from wine and strong drink, and \add even\add* vinegar made from wine or strong drink, plus they mustn’t drink grape juice or eat either grapes or raisins.
\v 4 For the entire time of their separation, they mustn’t eat anything coming from a grapevine, not even the seeds or the skins.
\p
\v 5 “For the entire time of their vow to Yahweh as a Nazirite, they mustn’t cut their hair—they must sacred and grow their hair long.
\p
\v 6 “For the entire time of their separation to Yahweh, they mustn’t approach any dead body,
\v 7 even if it’s their father or mother, or brother or sister. They mustn’t defile themselves with death, because their consecration to their god is on their head.
\v 8 They must remain holy to Yahweh for the whole time.
\p
\v 9 “However, if someone right next to them suddenly died, that would defile their consecrated head, and after the \add regular\add* seven days of purification, they would have to shave their head.
\v 10 Then on the eighth day, they’d have to take two doves or young pigeons to the priest at the entrance of the sacred tent,
\v 11 and he would take one for a sin offering and the other for a burnt offering—making atonement for their transgression with the corpse. Then they would consecrate their head \add again\add* that day
\v 12 and restart their days of separation, as well as bringing a one-year old male lamb as a guilt offering. (Their initial days of separation won’t count because they were made ‘unclean’.)
\p
\v 13 “Now these are the instructions for the Nazirites: Once their days of separation are fulfilled, \add someone\add* must take them to the entrance of the sacred tent
\v 14 and they will present their offerings to Yahweh—an unblemished, one-year old, male lamb as a burnt offering, and an unblemished, one-year old, female lamb as a sin offering, plus an unblemished ram as a peace/fellowship offering,
\v 15 along with a basket of flat breads, flour mixed with oil, and wafers spread with \add olive\add* oil, and their grain and drink offerings.
\v 16 The priest must present all that to Yahweh, and handle their sin offering and burnt offering.
\v 17 The ram and the basket of flat breads will be offered as a peace offering to Yahweh, and the priest will offer their grain and wine offerings.
\v 18 Then the Nazirite must shave their head of separation at the opening to the sacred tent, and taking the hair, must put it on the fire under their peace offering sacrifice.
\p
\v 19 “The priest will then take the boiled shoulder from the ram, and one flat bread roll and one wafer from the basket, and place them on the palms of that Nazirite with their consecrated head that’s been shaved.
\v 20 Then the priest will raise them as a raised offering to Yahweh. Those raised breads and the shoulder are now sacred and belong to the priest. Then the Nazirite can drink some wine.
\p
\v 21 Those are the instructions for the Nazirite who makes a solemn oath to Yahweh for their separation, plus what they’re able to give. They must do exactly what they promised as per these instructions.”
\s1 The priests’ prayer for blessing
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 23 “Tell Aaron and his sons that when the bless the Israelites, this is what they must say:
\q1
\v 24 May Yahweh bless you and protect you.
\q1
\v 25 May Yahweh give you prosperity and show grace toward you.
\q1
\v 26 May he watch over you and give you peace.
\p
\v 27 “In that way, they’ll put my name on the Israelites, and I will bless them.”
\c 7
\s1 The leaders’ offerings at the altar dedication
\p
\v 1 When Moses had finished setting up the sacred tent, he anointed it \add with oil\add* and consecrated it and all its furniture, as well as the altar and all its equipment.
\v 2 Then Israel’s leaders presented gifts. They were the leaders of the tribes who ruled over those who’d been listed \add for duties\add*.
\v 3 They brought their gifts to Yahweh’s presence in six carts \add pulled by\add* twelve bulls. Each cart was from two leaders, and each of the bulls was from one leader.
\v 4 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\v 5 “Accept their gifts because they’ll be useful for the running of the sacred tent. Distribute them appropriately to the Levites, depending on each man’s duties.”
\v 6 So Moses took the carts and the cattle and distributed them to the Levites,
\v 7 giving two carts and four bulls to the Gershonim to help them with their assigned duties.
\v 8 Then he gave four carts and eight bulls to the Merarim to help them with their assigned duties under the supervision of the priest (Aaron’s son) Itamar.
\v 9 However, the Kohatites weren’t given any because they were responsible for carrying the \add very\add* holy objects, and those had to be carried on their shoulders.
\p
\v 10 The day that the altar was dedicated and anointed, the leaders brought their gifts and presented them at the altar,
\v 11 and Yahweh told Moses, “Each day \add for the next twelve days\add*, one leader must present his offering for the altar dedication.”
\p
\v 12-83 These were the leaders who presented their offerings:
\li1 On the first day, Amminadab’s son Nahshon \add on behalf of\add* the Judah tribe,
\li1 on the second day, Tsuar’s son Netanel \add on behalf of\add* the Issachar tribe,
\li1 on the third day, Helon’s son Eliav \add on behalf of\add* the Zebulun tribe,
\li1 on the fourth day, Shedeyur’s son Elitsur \add on behalf of\add* the Reuben tribe,
\li1 on the fifth day, Tsurishadai’s son Shelumiel \add on behalf of\add* the Simeon tribe,
\li1 on the sixth day, Deuel’s son Elyasaf \add on behalf of\add* the Gad tribe,
\li1 on the seventh day, Amihud’s son Elishama \add on behalf of\add* the Ephraim tribe,
\li1 on the eighth day, Pedahtsur’s son Gamaliel \add on behalf of\add* the Manasseh tribe,
\li1 on the ninth day, Gid’oni’s son Avidan \add on behalf of\add* the Benjamin tribe,
\li1 on the tenth day, Amishaday’s son Ahiezer \add on behalf of\add* the Dan tribe,
\li1 on the eleventh day, Okran’s son Pagiel \add on behalf of\add* the Asher tribe,
\li1 and on the twelfth day, Eynan’s son Ahira \add on behalf of\add* the Naphtali tribe.
\p Each of the leaders presented the following offerings:
\li1 a silver dish (1.5 kilograms—all weights by the standard temple measurement) and a silver bowl (800g)—both of them full of fine flour mixed with oil as a grain offering,
\li1 a small gold dish (110g) filled with incense,
\li1 a young bull, a ram and a one-year-old male lamb for a burnt offering,
\li1 a male goat for a sin offering,
\li1 two cows, five rams, five male goats, and five one-year-old rams for the peace offering.
\p
\v 84 That was the dedication of the altar in the day it was anointed, when Israel’s leaders \add presented\add* twelve silver dishes, twelve silver bowls, and twelve gold pans.
\v 85 Each silver dish weighed 1.5 kilograms (all weights by the standard temple measurement) and the silver bowls were 800g. The total weight of the silver was almost thirty kilograms.
\v 86 The gold pans that had been filled with incense weighed 110g and their total weight came to 1.3 kilograms.
\v 87 For the burnt offerings there was: twelve bulls, twelve rams, and twelve male one-year-old lambs along with their grain offerings. There were twelve male goats for a sin offering.
\v 88 For the peace offering, there were 24 bulls, 60 rams, 60 male goats, 60 male one-year-old lambs. All of those were part of the dedication offerings for the altar after it was anointed.
\v 89 Then Moses entered the sacred tent to speak with Yahweh. He heard the voice speaking to him from between the two images of winged-creatures that were on the platform above the \add sacred\add* box. That’s how Yahweh spoke to him.
\c 8
\s1 Lighting the seven lamps
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell Aaron that when he places the seven lamps \add on\add* the lampstand, they must light up the area in front of it.”
\v 3 So Aaron did it just as Yahweh had instructed, setting up the lamps on the lampstand to face forwards.
\v 4 The lampstand from the base to its blossoms had been made by hammering \add a single sheet\add* of gold, just as Yahweh had shown Moses.
\s1 The dedication of Levites
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 6 “Out of all the Israelites, take the Levites and purify them.
\v 7 This is how to make them pure: sprinkle the sin offering water on them, then they must shave their entire bodies and wash themselves and their clothes.
\v 8 They must take a young bull along with its grain offering of flour mixed with oil, plus a second young bull for a sin offering.
\v 9 You must have the Levites stand at the front of the sacred tent and summon the rest of the Israelites \add there too\add*.
\v 10 Then you must present the Levites in front of Yahweh and the Israelite men must place their hands on them.
\v 11 Out of all the Israelites, Aaron must present the Levites as a raised offering to Yahweh so they can serve Yahweh \add with their assigned duties\add*.
\v 12 Then the Levites must place their hands on the bulls’ heads, and one must be given as a sin offering and the other as a burnt offering to Yahweh, to make the Levites innocent in Yahweh’s eyes.
\p
\v 13 “The Levites must stand in front of Aaron and his sons, then they can be offered as a raised offering to Yahweh.
\v 14 That’s how they’ll be distinguished from the other Israelites, and then they’ll belong to me.
\v 15 Then the Levites will go to serve at the sacred tent after you’ve purified them and presented them as a raised offering,
\v 16 because they’re given to me by all the Israelite people—I’ve taken them for myself in place of their firstborn male sons,
\v 17 because every firstborn Israelite male person and animal belongs to me. I set them apart for myself when I killed every \add other\add* firstborn in Egypt,
\v 18 so I’ve \add now\add* taken the Levites instead of the firstborn Israelite sons.
\v 19 Then I’ve given the Levites out of all the Israelites, to Aaron and his sons to serve the whole country by serving in the sacred tent and making the Israelites innocent in Yahweh’s eyes. That way, a deadly disease won’t strike the Israelites when they approach the sacred tent.”
\p
\v 20 So Moses and Aaron and all the Israelites did to the Levites \add just\add* what Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\v 21 The Levites purified themselves and washed their clothes, and Aaron offered them to Yahweh as a raised offering, and Aaron offered a sacrifice to make them innocent in Yahweh’s eyes.
\v 22 After that, the Levites went to do their \add assigned\add* duties at the sacred tent working under Aaron and his sons. The Israelites did exactly what Yahweh told Moses to do with the Levites.
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 24 “These are the instructions for the Levites: from the age of twenty-five, Levite men will begin serving the work in the sacred tent
\v 25 until the age of fifty when they must retire from that work.
\v 26 They may then assist their fellow Levites with guard duties, but they can no longer work \add directly\add* with the sacred tent.”
\c 9
\s1 The ‘pass-over’ celebration
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh spoke to Moses in the Sinai wilderness, in the first month of the second year after they’d left Egypt,
\v 2 “The Israelites must observe ‘pass-over’ at the appointed time.
\v 3 You all must celebrate it on the evening of the 14th of this month, following all the instructions that you’ve all been given previously.”
\v 4 So Moses ordered the Israelites to prepare the ‘pass-over’,
\v 5 and they celebrated it in the Sinai wilderness starting on the evening of the 14th of that first month, following all the instructions that Yahweh had given Moses.
\p
\v 6 However, there were some men who were ‘unclean’ (because they’d touched a dead body) and hence they weren’t able to celebrate the ‘pass-over’ that day, so they approached Moses and Aaron
\v 7 and asked them, “We were ‘unclean’ because we’d touched a dead body, \add but\add* how come we’re excluded from presenting an offering to Yahweh along with the other Israelites?”
\p
\v 8 “Wait here,” Moses answered, “and I’ll \add ask\add* Yahweh what he wants you all to do.”
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 10 “Tell the Israelites that for this and future generations, if a person is ‘unclean’ or far from home, then they can \add still\add* observe Yahweh’s ‘pass-over’.
\v 11 They can start it on the evening of the 14th of the \em second\em* month, and eat it \add as usual\add* with flat bread and bitter herbs.
\v 12 They must not break any of the lambs bones, must not leave any of it until the morning, and must observe it following all the normal instructions.
\v 13 However, any person who isn’t ‘unclean’ or far away, but yet who didn’t celebrate the ‘pass-over’ that first month, must be driven out of the community and will have to bear their \add own\add* sin.
\p
\v 14 If an outsider is living among you and celebrates the ‘pass-over’, they must also do it according to all the same instructions—there’s only one way to do it whether it’s you all, or a foreigner, or a native of the land.
\p
\v 15 When the sacred tent was dedicated, the cloud covered the inner room of the tent, and every evening it would look like fire above the residence, until morning \add came\add*.
\v 16 It remained like that continually—the cloud would cover it \add during the day\add*, and then at night, it would appear like fire.
\v 17 Whenever the cloud would be lifted up from the tent, then the Israelites would leave that place. \add Then\add* wherever the cloud settled down, that’s where the Israelites would set up camp,
\v 18 \add so in that way,\add* the Israelites would leave a place whenever Yahweh told them, and would set up camp whenever he told them. As long as the cloud stayed above the residence, they would \add stay\add* camped \add in that place\add*.
\v 19 Whenever the cloud remained above the residence for a long time, then the Israelites would follow Yahweh’s guidance and wouldn’t pack up camp.
\v 20 Sometimes the cloud would only remain above the residence for a few days, in which case they’d leave where they’d just camped, and move on as Yahweh commanded.
\v 21 If the cloud was lifted up the next morning, then they’d move on—\add in fact\add* whether day or night if the cloud was lifted up, then they would move on.
\v 22 Whether it was two days or a month or a year that the cloud stayed above the residence, then the Israelite camp would remain in that place, until \add eventually\add* moving on when it was lifted up \add again\add*.
\v 23 \add In that way,\add* when Yahweh ordered them to camp, they would, and when order to move on, they’d do that—thus they obeyed Yahweh’s instructions to Moses.
\c 10
\s1 The silver trumpets
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Make two hammered silver trumpets for yourself to use for assembling the community and for \add signalling when it’s time\add* to leave the camp.
\v 3 When both are sounded, the whole community is to assemble in front of you at the entrance to the sacred tent.
\v 4 If just one is sounded, then it’s \add only\add* the leaders and heads of clans who must assemble.
\v 5 A single blast means that the camps on the eastern side should \add start\add* moving out.
\v 6 A double blast means that the camps on the southern side should \add start\add* moving out. So the blasts will signal them to get going.
\v 7 (To assemble the community \add as above\add*, use long blows, not short blasts.)
\v 8 The priests (Aaron’s descendants) must be the ones to blow the trumpets. That is a permanent regulation.
\p
\v 9 When you need to defend yourselves against an attacker, then you must sound an alarm with the trumpets, and you’ll all be noticed by Yahweh, and you’ll be saved from your enemies.
\p
\v 10 On festival days and at times of celebrations and at the beginning of each new month, then the trumpets will be blown as the burnt offerings and peace offerings are made as a memorial for you all towards your god. I am your god, Yahweh.
\s1 The Israelites leave Sinai
\p
\v 11 Then on the 20th of the second month in that second year \add (after leaving Egypt\add* \add )\add*, the cloud was lifted up from above the sacred tent
\v 12 and the Israelites started moving out from the Sinai wilderness as per the pre-arranged order. The cloud moved to the Paran wilderness.
\v 13 That was the first time that they had moved as a result of Yahweh signalling Moses \add by means of the cloud\add*.
\p
\v 14 Judah tribe with their banner was the first to leave under the leadership of Amminadab’s son Nahshon.
\v 15 Tsuar’s son Netanel led the company from Issachar tribe,
\v 16 and Helon’s son Eliav led the company from Zebulun tribe.
\p
\v 17 Then the sacred tent was disassembled, and the men of Gereshon and Merari set off, carrying the tent.
\p
\v 18 Reuben tribe with their banner was next to leave under the leadership of Shedeyur’s son Elitsur.
\v 19 Tsurishadai’s son Shelumiel led the company from Simeon tribe.
\v 20 Deuel’s son Elyasaf led the company from Gad tribe.
\p
\v 21 Then the sacred utensils were carried out by the Kohatite \add men\add*, who then helped set up the sacred tent when they arrived.
\p
\v 22 Ephraim tribe with their banner was next to leave under the leadership of Amihud’s son Elishama.
\v 23 Pedahtsur’s son Gamaliel led the company from Manasseh tribe.
\v 24 Gid’oni’s son Avidan led the company from Benjamin tribe.
\p
\v 25 Dan tribe with their banner was last to leave as the rear guard under the leadership of Amishaday’s son Ahiezer.
\v 26 Okran’s son Pagiel led the company from Asher tribe.
\v 27 Eynan’s son Ahira led the company from Naphtali tribe.
\v 28 That was the order of the departures of the companies of the Israelite tribes when they set off.
\p
\v 29 Then Moses told Hovav (son of the Midianite Re’uel who was Moses’s father-in-law), “We’re heading towards the place that Yahweh said he’d give us. Come and join us, and we’ll look after you, because Yahweh has promised to look after Israel.”
\p
\v 30 “\add Thank you but\add* no,” Hovav replied, “I’ll return to my land and to my relatives.”
\p
\v 31 “Please don’t leave us,” Moses insisted, “because you’re \add the one who\add* knows about camping in the wilderness, and you could be our guide.
\v 32 When you come with us, Yahweh will be blessing us so we’ll also bless you.”
\p
\v 33 Then they left Yahweh’s mountain and travelled for three days, with the box containing Yahweh’s agreement being carried at the front to find a resting place for them,
\v 34 and Yahweh’s cloud was over them \add each\add* day as they set out from their stop.
\p
\v 35 As the box started moving, Moses would say, “Yahweh, take action and then your enemies will scatter, and the ones hating you will flee away from your presence.”
\v 36 Then \add at the end of the day\add* when it came to rest, he would say, “Yahweh, return \add to\add* the tens of thousands of Israelites.”
\c 11
\s1 Complaints and fire from Yahweh
\p
\v 1 One day, the people were complaining \add about their situation\add* and when Yahweh heard them, it made him angry so he sent a fire that burnt up the edges of their camp.
\v 2 The people cried out to Moses \add for help\add*, and he prayed to Yahweh, and the fire stopped,
\v 3 and they named that place Tav’erah \add (which means ‘burning’)\add* because Yahweh’s fire had burnt among them.
\s1 The choosing of seventy elders
\p
\v 4 However, there was a group of stirrers among the people that badly wanted to have meat and other foods to eat. That spread to all the Israelites who wept and asked, “Who’ll give us meat to eat?
\v 5 We remember the fish that we ate in Egypt for free, \add as well as\add* the cucumbers, melons, leeks, onions, and garlic,
\v 6 but now our appetite has shrivelled up because we don’t see anything to eat except this ‘stuff’.”
\p
\v 7 (The ‘stuff’ \add they ate every day\add* was \add a bit\add* like coriander seed \add except kind of clear\add*.
\v 8 \add Each morning\add* the people went around gathering it, then ground it with a \add stone\add* mill or pounded it in a mortar, then they boiled it and made something like bread rolls with it. They tasted like fresh \add olive\add* oil.
\v 9 The ‘stuff’ \add actually\add* descended on the camp during the night with the dew.)
\p
\v 10 Moses heard the people weeping in their tents (set up by tribe and clan). He thought it was wrong, and \add it made\add* Yahweh very angry.
\v 11 So Moses asked Yahweh, “Why have you caused me, your servant, to be in trouble \add like this\add*? Don’t you approve of me?. Why has the burden of all this people group \add landed\add* on me?
\v 12 Was it me who conceived it? Did I carry it to the land promised to their ancestors, like I was a wet-nurse?
\v 13 All these people come to me to ask me to give them meat to eat. Where would I get \add enough\add* meat \add to feed all\add* them?
\v 14 I can’t bear \add the load of\add* this entire people group by myself—it’s too heavy for me.
\v 15 If that’s what you’re doing to me, please kill me now if I’ve found favour in your eyes. Then I wouldn’t have to suffer like this.”
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Bring me seventy of Israel’s elders who you know are good leaders, and take them to the sacred tent, and let them take their positions there with you.
\v 17 I’ll come down and talk with you there, and I’ll take some of the spirit that’s on you and put it on them, and then they’ll carry the burden of the people along with you. Then you won’t \add have to\add* carry it by yourself.
\v 18 Also, tell the people that because they wept in my hearing about not having meat to eat like you all did in Egypt, they must purify themselves for tomorrow, because I’ll provide meat for you all to eat.
\v 19 You won’t \add just\add* have meat for a day or two, or for five, ten, or twenty days,
\v 20 you have it for a \add whole\add* month until it comes out your nostrils. \add Yes,\add* it’ll make you all nauseous because you rejected Yahweh who’s among you, and you all wept in front of him, complaining, ‘Why did we ever leave Egypt?’ ”
\p
\v 21 Moses queried, “There’s 600,000 able to serve in the army \add plus women and children\add*, yet you say that you’ll give them \add all\add* enough meat to eat \add every\add* day for month!
\v 22 Even if we killed all the cattle and sheep, or caught all the fish in the sea, that wouldn’t be enough meat for \add all\add* them.”
\p
\v 23 “\add Do you think that\add* Yahweh’s powerless?” Yahweh replied. “Soon you’ll see whether or not my statement will come true.”
\p
\v 24 So Moses went out and passed on Yahweh’s message to the people. Then he gathered seventy men from the Israelite elders and stood them around the tent.
\v 25 Then Yahweh came down in the cloud and spoke to Moses, and he took some of the spirit away from him and put it on those seventy elders. As the spirit rested on them, they prophesied (but they never did that again).
\p
\v 26 Two of the men who were listed (named Eldad and Medad), had remained in the \add main\add* camp (not going to the \add sacred\add* tent), and the spirit had \add also\add* rested on them and they prophesied in the camp.
\v 27 A young man ran to Moses and told him that Eldad and Medad were prophesying \add there\add* in the camp.
\p
\v 28 Then Nun’s son Joshua who had helped Moses since his youth) responded, “My master Moses, stop them \add from that\add*.”
\p
\v 29 But Moses replied, “Are you being jealous on my behalf? I’d be happy if Yahweh would put his spirit on \em all\em* the people and make them into prophets.”
\v 30 Then Moses and the elders went back to their tents.
\s1 Yahweh sends quails for meat
\p
\v 31 Then Yahweh sent a wind that blew quails from the ocean, and they dropped on the ground a day’s walk in every direction around the camp \add until they were piled\add* a metre high.
\v 32 So the people went out and collected the quail—each family getting at least the equivalent of ten large barrels full, and they spread the meat around the camp \add to sun dry\add*.
\v 33 Then when they started to \add cook and\add* eat \add some of the fresh\add* meat, Yahweh got very angry and struck the people with a terrible deadly disease before they could even chew it.
\v 34 So that place was named Kivrot-Hata’avah \add (which means ‘Graves of the cravers’)\add*, because that was where they buried the people who had craved meat.
\v 35 From Kivrot-Hata’avah, the people moved on to Hatserot and stayed there.
\c 12
\s1 Miriam’s punishment
\p
\v 1 One day, Miriam and Aaron spoke out against Moses because he’d taken an \add Ethiopian\add* woman (Kushit) as a wife,
\v 2 and they challenged, “Did Yahweh really only speak through Moses? Doesn’t he also speak through us?” Yahweh heard \add what they said\add*.
\v 3 (Now Moses was \add actually\add* a very humble man—more humble than anyone else alive.)
\p
\v 4 Then Yahweh suddenly told Moses, Aaron, and Miriam, “Go out, the three of you, to the sacred tent.” So the three of them went there.
\v 5 and Yahweh descended in a pillar of cloud and stood at the tent opening and called Aaron and Miriam, and the two of them stepped forward,
\v 6 and he said, “Listen to me. When there’s a prophet of Yahweh, I make myself known to him in a vision and speak to him in a dream,
\v 7 \add but\add* I don’t do that to my servant Moses. He’s the \add most\add* faithful of all my people
\v 8 \add and\add* I speak to him directly—face-to-face, and not in parables, and he’s able to see my form. So why weren’t you afraid to speak against my servant Moses?”
\p
\v 9 So Yahweh was angry at Miriam and Aaron. Then he departed
\v 10 and the cloud moved from the tent. Then wow, Miriam had leprosy—her skin had gone as white as snow. As Aaron turned to her, he saw, wow, she was leprous,
\v 11 and he begged Moses, “Please, my master, please don’t make us suffer for our foolish disrespect and disobedience.
\v 12 Don’t let her be like a still-born baby that’s already starting to decompose when its mother gives birth to it.
\p
\v 13 So Moses pleaded with Yahweh, “God, please, please heal her!”
\p
\v 14 “If her father had spit in her face, wouldn’t she stay inside in disgrace for seven days?” Yahweh replied. “\add So\add* she must be put outside the camp for seven days, then after that, she can enter again.”
\v 15 So Miriam was sent outside the camp for seven days, and the people didn’t move on until after she \add was allowed\add* to return.
\v 16 After that, the people left Hatserot and set up camp in the Paran wilderness.
\c 13
\s1 Twelve scouts sent into Canaan
\r (Deu. 1:19-33)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Send some men in to explore Canaan—the land that I’m giving to the Israelites. Send one man from each ancestral tribe—a leader within that tribe.”
\v 3 Then at Yahweh’s command, Moses sent them off from the Paran wilderness. They were all Israelite leaders
\v 4 and their names were:
\li1 Shamua (Zakur’s son) from Reuben tribe,
\li1
\v 5 Shafat (Hori’s son) from Simeon tribe,
\li1
\v 6 Caleb \add Caleb—\add*Yefuneh’s son) from Judah tribe,
\li1
\v 7 Yigal (Joseph’s son) from Issachar tribe,
\li1
\v 8 Hoshea (Nun’s son) from Ephraim tribe,
\li1
\v 9 Palti (Rafu’s son) from Benjamin tribe,
\li1
\v 10 Gadiel (Sodi’s son) from Zebulun tribe,
\li1
\v 11 Gadi (Susi’s son) from Joseph tribe,
\li1
\v 12 Amiyel (Gemali’s son) from Dan tribe,
\li1
\v 13 Setur (Michael’s son) from Asher tribe,
\li1
\v 14 Nahbi (Vofsi’s son) from Naphtali tribe, and
\li1
\v 15 Geuel (Maki’s son) from Gad tribe.
\p
\v 16 Moses called (Nun’s son) Hoshea, ‘Joshua’. Those were the names of the men that Moses sent out to explore Canaan.
\p
\v 17 So Moses sent them to explore Canaan, telling them, “Go through the Negev and then up into the hill country.
\v 18 Find out what the land’s like, and whether the people living there are \add militarily\add* strong or weak, and if they’re many or few.
\v 19 Find out whether the land is good or bad, what cities they live in, and how fortified they are.
\v 20 \add Find out\add* whether the soil is fertile or not, whether or not there’s trees there. Be courageous and bring back some of the produce from that land.” (He said that because it was the time \add of the year\add* when grapes were starting to ripen.)
\p
\v 21 So the twelve men went and explored the region from the Zin wilderness \add in the south, all the way\add* up to Rehov at Levo-Hamat \add in the north\add*.
\v 22 \add They started\add* in the Negev, and went as far as Hebron where some of Anak’s descendents lived: Ahiman, Sheshay, Talmay. Hebron had been built seven years before Tsoan in Egypt.
\v 23 When they got to the Eshkol riverbed, they cut off a branch with a cluster of grapes, and carried it on a pole between two of them, along with some pomegranates and figs.
\v 24 (That place was then called Eshkol, \add meaning ‘cluster’\add*, because of the cluster \add of grapes\add* they’d taken from there.)
\s2 The reports from the scouts
\p
\v 25 Forty days later, they returned from exploring that land
\v 26 and went to Kadesh in the Paran wilderness. There they reported back to Moses and Aaron and all the assembled people, and showed them the fruit from the land,
\v 27 and told them, “We went to the land where you sent us, and it’s certainly very fertile and this is its fruit.
\v 28 However, the people living in that land are powerful, and the cities are very well fortified. Also, some of the Anak people group live there.
\v 29 The Amalekite people live in the Negev, and the Hittites, Jebusites, and Amorites live in the hill country, and the Canaanites live along the coast and along the Jordan \add river\add*.”
\p
\v 30 Then Caleb \add raised an arm\add* to quieten the people in front of Moses and stated, “We’ll certainly go in there and take it over, because we’re capable of \add going\add* against them.”
\p
\v 31 But the men who’d gone in with him argued, “\add No\add*, we’re not able to \add battle\add* against them because they’re stronger than us.”
\v 32 Then \add they went on to\add* bring out a bad report to the Israelites about the land that they’d explored, saying, “The land that we passed to explore is a land that devours those who live in it. In addition, all the people we saw in there were tall \add and powerful\add*,
\v 33 plus we saw the Nefilim (descendants of Anak), and we felt \add as small as\add* grasshoppers compared to them, and that’s how they saw us as well.”
\c 14
\s1 The people rebel
\p
\v 1 The assembled \add people\add* cried loudly that entire night,
\v 2 and then they \add started\add* to protest against Moses and Aaron. They were all saying, “If only we’d died in Egypt or \add back there\add* in this wilderness.
\v 3 Why did Yahweh bring us to this place only to be slaughtered in battle with our wives and children being taken as plunder? Wouldn’t it be better for us to return to Egypt?”
\v 4 So they said to each other, “Let’s choose a new leader to take us back to Egypt.”
\p
\v 5 Then Moses and Aaron fell to their knees with their faces bowed in front of all the assembled Israelites.
\v 6 Then Nun’s son Joshua and Yefuneh’s son Caleb, two of those who’d explored that land, tore their clothes \add in grief\add*
\v 7 and spoke to all those Israelites gathered there, “The land that we passed throughout and explored is a very, very good place.
\v 8 If Yahweh is pleased with us, then he’ll take us into it and give it to us—that land that’s very fertile.
\v 9 However, you all mustn’t rebel against Yahweh, and you mustn’t be scared of the people in that area because they’ll \add be like\add* bread for us. There’s nothing left to protect them as Yahweh is with us, \add so\add* don’t be afraid.”
\v 10 Then the mass of people talked about killing them by throwing rocks at them, but Yahweh’s brilliant light \add suddenly\add* appeared to all the Israelites from the sacred tent.
\s1 Moses asks Yahweh to forgive
\p
\v 11 Yahweh said to Moses, “How long will this people group spurn me for? How long will they refuse to believe in me for, despite all the miracles that I’ve done among them?
\v 12 I’ll strike them with a deadly disease and destroy them. I’m make you into a nation that’s greater and more powerful than them.”
\p
\v 13 But Moses disagreed with Yahweh, “Then Egypt will hear about how you used your power to bring these people out from among them,
\v 14 and they’ll report to the inhabitants of Canaan, because those inhabitants have heard that \add you\add*, Yahweh, are \add right here\add* in the middle of us Israelite people, and how your cloud rests on us, and how you lead us with that pillar of cloud that becomes a pillar of fire at night.
\v 15 If you exterminate all the Israelites, those nations who’ve heard about your fame will say,
\v 16 ‘\add That\add* Yahweh couldn’t take those people into the land that he promised them, so he slaughtered them in the wilderness.’
\v 17 So may the power of my master be great, just as you’ve \add previously\add* said,
\v 18 ‘Yahweh is slow to anger and quick to show loyal commitment, forgiving disobedience and rebellion. Yet he doesn’t leave the guilty unpunished, bringing the consequences of the father’s disobedience onto the next three generations.’
\v 19 Please forgive this people for their disobedience, applying your incredible loyal commitment like how you did when you brought these people from Egypt all the way to here.”
\p
\v 20 “I’ve forgiven as per your request,” Yahweh replied,
\v 21 “however, as surely as I live and as all the land is filled with Yahweh’s light,
\v 22 and despite them all seeing my power and the miracles that I did \add both\add* in Egypt and in the wilderness, they’ve \add now\add* tested me many times and haven’t done what I told them,
\v 23 so there’s no way that they’ll enter the land that I promised to their ancestors. All those who despised me won’t \add get to\add* enter it.
\v 24 However, my servant Caleb has a different spirit with him, and he wholeheartedly followed me, so I’ll take him into the land that he explored, and his descendants will inherit it.
\v 25 The Amalekites and Canaanites live in the valleys. Tomorrow, you all must turn and set off towards the wilderness via the route to the Red sea.
\s1 Punishment for the protestors
\p
\v 26 Then Yahweh said to Moses and Aaron,
\v 27 “How much longer \add do I have to put up with\add* this evil gathering that grumble against me? I’ve heard the complaints that the Israelites are making against me.
\v 28 Tell them \add this\add*, ‘Yahweh declares that as I live, your own words that I heard are exactly what I’ll do to you all.
\v 29 \add Indeed\add* you’ll die there in the wilderness—all those men who were listed as being twenty years old and older that complained against me.
\v 30 You all won’t enter that land that I \add would have\add* used my power to settle you into. \add The only exceptions are\add* Yefuneh’s son Caleb and Nun’s son Joshua.
\v 31 The children of you all that you said would \add be taken\add* as plunder, I take \em them\em* in and they’ll experience the land that you all have rejected,
\v 32 but your bodies will remain in this wilderness.
\v 33 Your sons will become shepherds in the wilderness for forty years, and they will suffer for your unfaithfulness until you \add yourselves\add* all become corpses in the wilderness.
\v 34 For each day that you explored the land, you’ll all bear your disobedience for one year, so forty days will be forty years of experiencing my displeasure.
\v 35 I, Yahweh, have spoken that I’ll certainly do that to this evil community that joined together against me. They’ll meet their end and die there in the wilderness.’ ”
\p
\v 36 Now those \add ten\add* men who Moses had sent in to explore the land, and who’d returned and the made the whole assembly grumble against him because of their evil report about the land,
\v 37 they died from a deadly disease in front of Yahweh after producing that evil report.
\v 38 However, Joshua (\add Joshua\add*—Nun’s son) and Caleb (\add Caleb\add*—Yefuneh’s son) who had also explored that land, continue to live on.
\s1 The wrong-minded attempt to invade
\r (Deu. 1:41-46)
\p
\v 39 So Moses passed on to the Israelites what Yahweh had said, and the people were extremely sad.
\v 40 Early the \add next\add* morning, they went up towards the hilltops, saying, “Look at us all here! Yes, we sinned \add yesterday, but today\add* we’ll go up to the place that Yahweh said.”
\p
\v 41 “What’s that?” Moses responded. “Don’t go against Yahweh’s instructions because your plan won’t succeed.
\v 42 Don’t continue onwards because Yahweh isn’t with you and \add for sure\add* you’ll all be defeated by your enemies
\v 43 because you’ll all be facing the Amalekites and the Canaanites and you’ll die by the sword. Yes, you’ve turned away from following Yahweh so he won’t be with you all.”
\p
\v 44 \add Nevertheless,\add* they dared to continue up to the tops of the hills, but \add neither\add* Moses nor the box containing the agreement with Yahweh, left the camp.
\v 45 Then the Amalekites and Canaanites that lived in that \add part of the\add* hill country attacked them and killed them as far down as Hormah.
\c 15
\s1 Instructions about sacrifices and offerings
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that when they enter the region that I’ll give them to live in, \add they must do this:\add*
\v 3 \add When\add* you all make a gift to Yahweh—a burnt offering or a sacrifice, to fulfill a vow, or as a freewill offering, or at your appointed times, to make a scent of pleasing for Yahweh from the cattle or from the flock—
\v 4 then the donor must present their offering to Yahweh, \add along with\add* a grain offering of a kilogram of fine flour mixed with 0.9 litres of \add olive\add* oil.
\v 5 Also about a quarter of a litre of wine for the drink offering \add must be offered\add* with the burnt offering and/or with \add each\add* male lamb.
\v 6 For a ram, there must also be a grain offering of two kilograms of fine flour mixed with 1.2 litres of oil
\v 7 plus 1.2 litres of wine, all to be presented as a pleasing aroma to Yahweh.
\v 8 When you offer a young bull as a burnt offering or as a sacrifice such as fulfilling a vow or as a peace offering,
\v 9 the donor must present a grain offering of 3.5 kilograms of fine flour mixed with two litres of oil,
\v 10 plus a drink offering of two litres of wine, all to be presented as a pleasing aroma to Yahweh.
\p
\v 11 That’s what must be done for each bull or ram or lamb or young goat.
\v 12 Whatever number \add of animals\add* that you offer, there must be the appropriate \add grain and wine offerings\add*.
\v 13 Every native-born \add Israelite\add* must do that when presenting a gift, all to be presented as a pleasing aroma to Yahweh.
\p
\v 14 If a foreigner is staying with you all or living among you for \add multiple\add* generations, and they make an offering to Yahweh with a pleasing aroma, then they must follow the same instructions as all of you.
\v 15 \add Yes,\add* the same regulations apply to both you and the foreigners living among you all. That will always apply—foreigners in front of Yahweh must do the same as all of you.
\v 16 One \add set of\add* instructions and regulations for \add both\add* you and the foreigner that lives among you all.
\p
\v 17 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 18 “Tell the Israelites that when you get to the land that I’m taking you to,
\v 19 and \add you all settle there and\add* start eating food that you’ve grown there, you all must dedicate a \add special\add* contribution to Yahweh.
\v 20 You must present the first portion of your dough and hold it up \add just\add* like a presentation from the threshing floor.
\v 21 You all must do that \add each year\add* with your first dough, even through \add future\add* generations.
\s1 Handling unintentional non-compliance
\p
\v 22 “However, if you do something wrong and don’t follow all these instructions that Yahweh told Moses,
\v 23 i.e., don’t follow everything that Yahweh has commanded through Moses from when the commands were given \add right\add* through to future generations,
\v 24 then assuming that it was an accident, the community must offer a young bull for a burnt offering to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh, along with its grain offering and drink offering as per the instructions, plus a male goat as a sin offering.
\v 25 \add That way\add* the priest will make atonement for the Israelite community and they’ll be forgiven because it was a mistake and they’d brought their gift as an offering to Yahweh plus their sin offering because of their mistake.
\v 26 The Israelite community will be forgiven along with any foreigner living among them because it accidentally affected the entire community.
\p
\v 27 “Now if a single person disobeys by mistake, then that person must present a one-year-old female goat as a sin offering,
\v 28 and the priest will make atonement for that person. \add In that way,\add* the one making a mistake in front of Yahweh will be forgiven.
\v 29 \add Both\add* native-born Israelites and foreigners living among them must \add all\add* follow the same instructions after making a mistake.
\v 30 However, any person who disobeys defiantly, whether native-born or foreigner, they are slandering Yahweh and that person must be driven out of the community
\v 31 because they’ve despised Yahweh’s message and disobeyed his instructions. That person must be completely cut off and their guilt \add remains\add* on them.
\s1 Violating the rest day
\p
\v 32 \add One day\add* when the Israelites were in the wilderness, they noticed a man gathering firewood on the rest day,
\v 33 and those who caught him took him to Moses and Aaron and the rest of the community.
\v 34 They put him into custody, because it hadn’t been decided what to do with him,
\v 35 and Yahweh told Moses, “That man must definitely be put to death—the community must do it outside the camp by throwing rocks at him.”
\v 36 So the community took him outside the camp, and they executed him by throwing rocks at him just as Yahweh had commanded Moses.
\s1 Tassels on clothes
\p
\v 37 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 38 “Tell the Israelites that they must make tassels on the corners of their \add outer\add* robes. Each tassel must have a blue cord, and they must do that for all future generations.
\v 39 Then when you see a tassel, it’ll remind you of Yahweh’s instructions, and \add so\add* you’ll follow them rather than your own desires and lusts that lead you all into unfaithfulness.
\v 40 \add Instead,\add* you’ll remember and follow my instructions, and stay pure for your god.
\v 41 I am your god Yahweh—the one who brought you all out of Egypt to be your god. I am Yahweh your god.”
\c 16
\s1 Korah’s rebellion
\p
\v 1 Now Korah (son of Yitshar, son of Kohat, son of Levi) \add along with\add* Datan and Aviram (sons of Eliav) and On (son of Pelet)
\v 2 rebelled against Moses along with 250 respected, Israelite community leaders.
\v 3 They got together and challenged Moses and Aaron, “You think you’re great but the entire community is sacred and has Yahweh among us, so why do you consider yourselves more important than Yahweh’s community?”
\p
\v 4 When Moses heard that, he fell to his knees with his face to the ground
\v 5 and responded to Korah and his supporters, “\add Tomorrow\add* morning Yahweh will show who’s sacred and who belongs to him. Yahweh will present his chosen leader. make known who is to him and the holy one, and he will present to him, and whom he chooses he will present to him.
\v 6 So Korah and supporters, bring incense burners
\v 7 and put burning coals and incense in them, and bring them to Yahweh’s presence tomorrow. Then the man that Yahweh chooses will be the sacred one. It’s you Levites who think you’re great.”
\p
\v 8 Then Moses told Korah, “You Levites, please listen.
\v 9 Isn’t it enough for you all that Israel’s god separated you from the \add rest of\add* the Israelite community to present you to him to serve in Yahweh’s residence and to stand in front of the gatherings to minister to them?
\v 10 Yahweh has presented you and your brothers, the \add other\add* Levites to approach him. Are you all wanting the priesthood \add now\add* also?
\v 11 So it’s Yahweh that you and your group are opposing, not \add really\add* Aaron that you’re all grumbling about.”
\p
\v 12 Then Moses summoned Eliav’s sons Datan and Aviram but they refused, “We won’t come.
\v 13 Isn’t it enough that you brought us \em out\em* of a very fertile land, to kill us \add here\add* in the wilderness, without also making yourself the ruler over us?
\v 14 What’s more, you didn’t bring us into a very fertile land or give us an inheritance of land and vineyards. Will you gouge out the eyes of those Canaanites \add so that we can defeat them\add*? \add No,\add* we won’t come.”
\p
\v 15 \add That\add* made Moses very angry and he told Yahweh, “Don’t accept their grain offering. I’ve \add never\add* taken \add even\add* one donkey from them or done anything bad to any of them.”
\p
\v 16 Then Moses told Korah, “You and all your group, must appear in front of Yahweh tomorrow, as will Aaron.
\v 17 \add Each\add* man must bring his pan with incense burning in it and present it to Yahweh—all 250 of you as well as Aaron.
\v 18 So \add the next day,\add* they all brought their pans with burning coals and incense in them, and they stood at the entrance of the sacred tent, as did Moses and Aaron.
\v 19 plus Korah had \add also\add* assembled the entire community to oppose Moses and Aaron at the entrance to the sacred tent. Then Yahweh’s brilliance appeared to all the community
\v 20 and Yahweh warned Moses and Aaron,
\v 21 “Get away from all those people, then I can destroy them in an instant.”
\p
\v 22 However, Moses and Aaron fell to their knees with their faces to the ground and interceded, “God, \add you are\add* the god who gives life to everything. Will you be angry at the entire community \add because of\add* the sin of one man?”
\v 23 So Yahweh told Moses,
\v 24 “Tell the people to keep back from the homes of Korah, Datan, and Aviram.”
\p
\v 25 Then Moses walked to \add the homes of\add* Datan and Aviram, and the Israelite elders followed behind him,
\v 26 and he told the people, “Please keep back from the tents of these wicked men, and don’t touch anything of theirs, in case you get caught up in their disobedience.”
\v 27 So the rest of the Israelites kept back from the residences of Korah, Datan, and Aviram. Meanwhile, Datan and Aviram had come out of their tents, and were standing at the entrances with their wives and children.
\p
\v 28 Then Moses said, “This is how \add you’ll all\add* known that \add it was\add* Yahweh who selected me for this role—it’s \add never\add* been something that I aspired to:
\v 29 If all these men die a natural death like most people, then Yahweh didn’t choose me,
\v 30 but if Yahweh does something new and the ground opens its mouth and swallows them and their homes and tents and all their possessions, and if they then go down alive into their grave, then you’ll all know that it was because these men rejected Yahweh.”
\p
\v 31 As soon as Moses had finished speaking, the ground below those men split open
\v 32 and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed them and their homes and every man who backed Korah along with all their property.
\v 33 They went down alive to their grave, along with everything they owned, and then the ground covered over them and they vanished from the community.
\v 34 All the people who were around them fled \add away\add* screaming because they were afraid of getting swallowed by the earth as well.
\p
\v 35 Then Yahweh sent fire that consumed the 250 men who were presenting incense.
\s1 The incense burners
\p
\v 36 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 37 “Tell the priest Eleazar (Aaron’s son) to collect the incense pans from those who were burnt, and scatter the coals far away because they’re sacred.
\v 38 The pans of those who rebelled at the expense of their own lives must be hammered into thin plates to be attached to the altar, because they presented them in front of Yahweh and \add now\add* they’re sacred, and they’ll become a reminder for the Israelites.
\v 39 So the priest Eleazar took the bronze incense burners that had been presented by those men who’d been burnt up, and they were hammered into a plating for the altar
\v 40 for a memorial for the Israelites just as Yahweh had told him via Moses. That’s a reminder that no one who’s not a descendant of Aaron is allowed to approach Yahweh with incense burners, so they don’t end up like Korah and his supporters.
\s1 The people complain and cause a plague
\p
\v 41 However, the next day the Israelites complained about Moses and Aaron, “It was you who killed Yahweh’s people.”
\v 42 But as the community gathered \add there\add* against Moses and Aaron, they looked across to the sacred tent, and wow, the cloud covered over it and Yahweh’s brilliance appeared.
\v 43 Moses and Aaron went \add over\add* to the front of the sacred tent,
\v 44 and Yahweh told Moses,
\v 45 “Get away from this gathering and then I’ll finish them in an instant.”
\p But Moses and Aaron fell to their knees with their faces to the ground,
\v 46 and Moses told Aaron, “Take the incense burner, and put fire in it from the altar and incense, and take it quickly to the people and make atonement for them, because Yahweh’s rage is \add already\add* burning—the deadly disease has begun.”
\v 47 So Aaron took it as Moses had told him and ran into the middle of the people. Wow, the deadly disease had already begun, so he burnt incense and made atonement for them.
\v 48 He stood between those who were dying and those still alive, and the deadly disease was restrained.
\v 49 There were 14,700 people who died from the deadly disease, in addition to those who’d died due to Korah’s rebellion.
\v 50 Then Aaron returned to Moses at the sacred tent entrance, as the disease had been stopped.
\c 17
\s1 The budding of Aaron’s rod
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites to bring you twelve staffs—one from each ancestral \add tribe\add* with the leader’s name inscribed on it,
\v 3 and inscribe Aaron’s name on the staff for the Levites so there’s one for each tribe.
\v 4 Put them \add all\add* in the sacred tent in front of the sacred box where I meet with you,
\v 5 and then the staff of the man that I choose \add to be the priest\add* will sprout. Then the people can stop complaining to me about you.”
\p
\v 6 So Moses passed the message on to the Israelite leaders and they gave him one staff for each leader—a total of twelve including Aaron’s one,
\v 7 and Moses laid them in front of Yahweh in the sacred tent.
\p
\v 8 Then the next day when Moses went to the sacred tent, wow, he saw that Aaron’s staff (representing Levi tribe) had sprouted, plus it had blossomed and produced a flower and ripened almonds!
\v 9 Moses took all the staffs out of the tent to the people to look at and each of the leaders took back his own staff,
\v 10 then Yahweh told Moses, “Put Aaron’s staff \add back\add* in front of the sacred chest. Keep it there as a reminder to \add any\add* rebels to stop complaining against me so they won’t die.”
\v 11 So Moses did what Yahweh had commanded.
\p
\v 12 Then the Israelites complained to Moses, “Wow, we’re done for—it’s clear that we’re all going to die!
\v 13 Everyone who goes close to Yahweh’s residence gets killed. Won’t we all die?”
\c 18
\s1 The work of priests and Levites
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Aaron, “You and your Levite brothers and cousins and \add all\add* your descendants must bear the imperfections of the sacred tent, and you and your sons must bear the imperfections of your priesthood.
\v 2 Bring your Levite brothers (your father’s tribe) to work closely with you and to assist you \add as\add* you and your sons perform your duties at the sacred tent.
\v 3 They must look after the tent, however, they mustn’t approach the furnishings or utensils, or the altar, so that both you and they won’t die.
\v 4 Then must work closely with you and take care of all the duties to do with the sacred tent, but no outsider is allowed to approach.
\v 5 You \add priests\add* will take care of the sacred place and the altar, and then there won’t be anymore fury against the Israelite people.
\v 6 Now listen, I’ve taken your brothers the Levites out from the rest of the Israelites as a gift for you—they’re given to do the duties at the sacred tent.
\v 7 However, it’s you and your sons who are the priests who must serve for things to do with the altar and inside the curtain. Your priesthood is a gift of service, and any outsider who approaches must be executed.”
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Aaron, “Also listen, I’ve put you in charge of looking after the sacred offerings to me. I’ve given them to you and your sons as your permanent income.
\v 9 Every offering apart from the burnt offerings, will belong to you. That includes their grain offerings, their sin offerings, and their guilt offerings. Once they’re given, they become sacred, and they’re for you and your sons.
\v 10 You and the males in your families may eat that sacred offering but do remember that it’s sacred.
\p
\v 11 “In addition, the \add people’s\add* raised offerings belong to you, and they are permanently given to your entire families to eat—everyone who’s ‘clean’ may eat them.
\p
\v 12 “All the best of the fresh oil, and the new wine and grain that they give to Yahweh, I give to you.
\v 13 The first portions of all their harvests that they bring to Yahweh, belong to you—everyone in your families who’s ‘clean’ may eat them.
\p
\v 14 “Everything throughout Israel that’s dedicated \add to Yahweh\add* will belong to you.
\p
\v 15 “Every firstborn \add male\add* baby, as well as animals, will belong to Yahweh, \add however\add* you must buy back the human babies as well as the firstborns from the ‘unclean’ livestock.
\v 16 The babies must be bought back when they’re one month old, paying five silver coins of the proper weight according to the scales at the sacred tent.
\v 17 However, the firstborn cows or sheep or goats can’t be bought back. They are sacred—you must sprinkle their blood on the altar and burn their fat as an offering that’s a soothing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 18 Their meat will belong to you, just like the breast or the right thigh of the wave offerings are yours.
\v 19 All sacred donations by the Israelites, I’ve permanently given to you and your children to eat—it’s an irrevocable agreement with Yahweh for you and your \add future\add* ancestors.”
\s1 The Levites’ share
\p
\v 20 Yahweh \add also\add* told Aaron, “You \add priests\add* won’t inherit any land or property—I am your share and your inheritance among the Israelites.
\p
\v 21 For an inheritance for the Levites, I’ve given them all the tenths given by the Israelites as their inheritance in exchange for their service at the sacred tent.
\v 22 The other Israelites mustn’t approach that tent \add or\add* they’ll incur guilt and die,
\v 23 but the Levites must serve at the sacred tent and they must bear their inadequacies. That order applies for all future generations, including the not inheriting any land like the other Israelites.
\v 24 \add That’s\add* because I’ve given the Levites that tenth that the other Israelites offer to Yahweh as a donation. That’s why I said that they won’t receive any other inheritance.”
\s1 The Levites’ contribution
\p
\v 25 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 26 “Tell the Levites: ‘When you all receive the tenth from the Israelites that I’ve given to you as you inheritance, then you must offer a tenth of that tenth as a donation to Yahweh.
\v 27 That will be considered as your contribution \add just\add* as if it was grain from the threshing floor, or juice from the wine-pressing \add pit\add*.
\v 28 So when you all offer that donation from all the tenths that you receive from the Israelites, it must then be given as Yahweh’s contribution to the priest Aaron. DOUBLE-CHECK
\v 29 When an offering is presented to Yahweh, it must be the best parts of whatever is given.’
\v 30 Also tell them, ‘When you all present the best of what was given, it’ll be as it’s come straight from the threshing floor or the wine-pressing \add pit\add*.
\v 31 \add The rest\add* is for your wages for the service you all do at the sacred tent, and you all can eat it wherever you want to.
\v 32 After donating the best tenth from it, you won’t incur guilt for what you all do with the rest of it, however you all mustn’t defile any sacred things or you’ll die.’ ”
\c 19
\s1 The red cow’s ashes
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron,
\v 2 “This is a new law that Yahweh has commanded, ‘Tell the Israelites that they must bring you an unblemished red heifer with no defects and that has never had a yoke on its neck.
\v 3 Give it to the priest Eleazar, and he must take it outside the camp and have \add someone\add* slaughter it in front of him.
\v 4 Then Eleazar must get some of its blood with his finger seven times and flick it onto the ground directly in front of the sacred tent.
\v 5 Then while he watches, they must \add completely\add* burn the heifer—its skin and flesh, and \add including\add* its blood and dung.
\v 6 Meanwhile, the priest must take some cedar wood, hyssop, and scarlet wool, and throw them into \add the fire\add* where the cow is burning.
\v 7 Then the priest must wash his clothes and bathe. After that, he can return to the camp, but he’ll be ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\v 8 The \add man\add* who burnt the cow must \add also\add* wash his clothes and bathe, and he will \add also\add* be ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\v 9 A ‘clean’ man must collect the cow’s ashes and place them in a sacred place outside the camp where they can be used for water for purification for the Israelites to purify from disobedience.
\v 10 Then that man who collected the ashes must \add also\add* wash his clothes and be ‘unclean’ until that evening. Those regulations will permanently apply both to Israelites and to foreigners living among them.
\s1 Contact with a corpse
\p
\v 11 “ ‘All those who touch a dead, human body will be ‘unclean’ for seven days.
\v 12 On the third day, they must purify themselves, and then they’ll be ‘clean’ on the seventh day. (If they don’t purify themselves, they’ll won’t become ‘clean’ on that seventh day—
\v 13 if they’ve touched a dead, human body and don’t purify themselves, that would defile Yahweh’s residence, and \add so\add* they must be permanently removed from the camp. If the purifying water wasn’t sprinkled on them, that ‘uncleanness’ will remain on them.)
\p
\v 14 “ ‘This is the regulation if a person dies in a tent: Everyone who was in the tent, and everyone that enters \add afterwards\add*, will all be ‘unclean’ for seven days,
\v 15 and every container that doesn’t have its lid on becomes ‘unclean’.
\v 16 Out in the countryside, who touches the corpse of someone killed in a fight, or any dead human body or bone or grave, will be ‘unclean’ for seven days.
\v 17 Someone must take some of the ashes from that burnt cow, and put them into a jar with some fresh, running water, then \add pour some of it over them\add*.
\v 18 Then a man who’s ‘clean’ must dip some hyssop in that water and sprinkle it onto the tent and all the containers \add in it\add* and everyone who lives in it or who touched the body or the bone or grave.
\v 19 On the third day, that ‘clean’ man must sprinkle that water on the ‘unclean’ ones, and then must purify them on the seventh day. Then he \add himself\add* must wash his clothes and bathe, and will be ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\p
\v 20 “ ‘Anyone who’s ‘unclean’ and doesn’t purify themself, that person will be permanently removed from the camp because they’ve made Yahweh’s sanctuary ‘unclean’. They didn’t sprinkle that purifying water on themself, so they \add remain\add* ‘unclean’.
\v 21 That will be a permanent regulation. The person who’s had the purifying water sprinkled on them must wash their clothes, and any person who sprinkles the water will be ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\v 22 Anything touched by an ‘unclean’ person becomes ‘unclean’, and anyone who touches an ‘unclean’ person will become ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\c 20
\s1 Water from the Kadesh rock
\r (Exo. 17:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Then in the first month \add of the next year\add*, all the Israelites entered the Zin \add ‘Thorny’ or ‘Thirsty’)\add* wilderness and the people camped at Kadesh. \add While they were\add* there, Miriam died, and was buried there.
\p
\v 2 However, there was no water to drink, so the people came against Moses and Aaron
\v 3 and complained, “It would have been better if we’d died in front of Yahweh along with our relatives \add back there\add*.
\v 4 Why did you two bring Yahweh’s people here to die in this wilderness, along with our livestock?
\v 5 Why did you bring us out of Egypt to bring us to this miserable place? There’s no grain or figs, and no grapes or pomegranates. There’s \add not even\add* water to drink!”
\v 6 Then Moses and Aaron went from \add there\add* in front of the people to the entrance to the sacred tent, where they fell to their knees with their faces to the ground, and Yahweh’s brilliance appeared to them.
\p
\v 7 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 8 “Take Aaron’s staff, then the two of you must command \add that\add* rock to pour out water. Then water will come out of it, and both the people and the animals will \add have plenty to\add* drink.”
\p
\v 9 So Moses took the staff from Yahweh’s residence like he was told to.
\v 10 Then the two of them gathered the people in front of the rock and told them, “Listen now you bunch of rebels—do we have to bring water out of this rock for you?”
\v 11 Then Moses raised the staff and hit the rock twice, and water gushed out, and so the people and their animals were able to drink.
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh scolded Moses and Aaron, “Because you two publicly demonstrated that you didn’t believe that what I say is absolute truth, therefore you two won’t lead this community into the land that I’ve given them.”
\p
\v 13 That spring was named ‘Merivah’ \add (meaning ‘Quarrelling’)\add* because that was where the Israelites argued with Yahweh, and where he demonstrated his absolute truthfulness to them.
\s1 Edom’s king refuses the Israelites’ passage
\p
\v 14 Then from there in Kadesh, Moses sent messengers to Edom’s king to ask him, “This \add message is from\add* your Israelite relatives. You are already aware of all the hardship that we’ve been through—
\v 15 \add how\add* our ancestors went down to Egypt and lived there for many years, but then the Egyptians mistreated them.
\v 16 However, \add when\add* we cried out to Yahweh and he heard us and sent a messenger and brought us out of Egypt, and now we’re in Kadesh town at the \add southern\add* border of your territory.
\v 17 \add Now\add* please let us pass through your country. We won’t go through the countryside or the vineyards, and we won’t drink water from any well. We’ll stay on the king’s highway without turning to the right or the left, until after we’ve exited at your \add northern\add* border.
\p
\v 18 But Edom’s \add king\add* replied, “You can’t pass through my country. If you do try to enter, I’ll send my swordsmen out against you.”
\p
\v 19 The Israelites reiterated, “We’ll stay on the highway, and if we or our livestock drink \add any of\add* your water, then we’ll pay what it’s worth so that’s no big deal. Just let us walk through.”
\p
\v 20 Again the king responded, “You may not pass through.”
\p Then Edom sent a large, heavily-armed contingent to intimidate them,
\v 21 and because they’d been refused passage through their territory, the Israelites turned around \add and went via a longer, less direct route\add*.
\s1 Aaron’s death
\p
\v 22 So the Israelites left Kadesh and went to Mt. Hor.
\v 23 There near the Edom border, Yahweh told Aaron and Moses,
\v 24 “Aaron will be taken to join his ancestors because he won’t not enter the land that I have given to the Israelites, because you two rebelled against what I said at the Merivah rock that gave water.
\v 25 Take Aaron and his son Eleazar and climb up Mt. Hor \add together\add*,
\v 26 then remove his high priest’s uniform and put it on his son Eleazar. After that, Aaron will die there and go to join his ancestors.”
\p
\v 27 So Moses followed Yahweh’s instructions and the three of them climbed Mt. Hor as the people watched.
\v 28 Then Moses removed Aaron’s uniform and put it on his son Eleazar. Aaron died there at the top of the mountain, and then Moses and Eleazar went \add back\add* down.
\v 29 When the assembled people realised that Aaron had died, all the Israelites mourned for thirty days for him.
\c 21
\s1 The defeat of Canaanites
\p
\v 1 When Canaanite king of Arad who lived by the wilderness, heard that Israel was coming on the Atarim road, he attacked them and captured some prisoners.
\v 2 Then the Israelites promised Yahweh, “If you help us defeat them, then we’ll completely destroy their towns (instead of looting them) as a way of offering them to you.”
\v 3 Yahweh listened to what they said and enabled them to defeat those Canaanites, then they dedicated the towns \add to Yahweh\add* and destroyed them, and they named that area ‘Hormah’ \add (which means ‘Destruction’)\add*.
\p
\v 4 Then they \add all\add* left Mt. Hor by the road going towards the Red sea to go around Edom, but the people became impatient on the way
\v 5 and they started to grumble against God and against Moses, “Why did you two bring us out of Egypt \add just\add* to die in the wilderness, because there’s no bread and no water, and our insides detest that tasteless ‘stuff’?”
\v 6 So Yahweh sent poisonous snakes into the people, and many Israelites were bitten and died.
\p
\v 7 The people came to Moses and begged, “We’ve done wrong by speaking out against Yahweh and against you. Pray to Yahweh \add for us\add* to get him to take the snakes away.” So Moses prayed on the peoples’ behalf,
\v 8 and Yahweh told him, “Make \add a model of\add* a snake and put it \add up\add* on a pole, and so that anyone who gets bitten, can see it and live.”
\v 9 So Moses made a snake out of bronze and attached it \add to the top\add* of a pole, and anyone who was bitten by a snake would recover when they looked towards the bronze snake.
\s1 Going around Edom via Moab
\p
\v 10 Then the Israelites set off and camped at Ovot.
\v 11 When they left Ovot, they camped at Iyey-Ha’avarim in the wilderness near Moab’s eastern border.
\v 12 From there they set off and camped by the Zered riverbed,
\v 13 before moving on and camping at \add the north side of\add* the Arnon \add river\add*, in the wilderness that extends from the Amorite border. (The Arnon is the border between Moab and the Amorites.
\v 14 That’s why it says in the book about Yahweh’s battles:
\q1 “Vahev in Sufah, and the riverbeds there, and the Arnon river
\q1
\v 15 and the sloping valleys there,
\q1 that extend to Ar village along Moab’s border.”)
\p
\v 16 From there they went to the well at Be’er \add (which means ‘Well’)\add*, where Yahweh told Moses, “Gather the people, and I’ll give them water.”
\v 17 That’s where the Israelites sang this song:
\q1 “Come on, well,
\q1 sing for this well.
\q1
\v 18 the well dug by the leaders,
\q1 the top citizens dug it out.
\q1 with their sceptres and their staffs.”
\p From that wilderness, \add the people went on\add* to Matanah,
\v 19 then through Nahaliel and on to Bamot
\v 20 and from there to the valley in the Moab countryside where Mt. Pisgah rises above the desert.
\s1 Kings Sihon and Og defeated
\r (Deu. 2:26–3:11)
\p
\v 21 Then Israel sent messengers to the Amorite King Sihon,
\v 22 “Let us pass through your country. We won’t turn off into any field or vineyard. We won’t take water from any well. We’ll stay on the king’s highway until we’ve exited at your \add northern\add* border.”
\v 23 However \add King\add* Sihon refused them permission, then he took his whole army into the wilderness to meet Israel, and they attacked them at Yahats \add village\add*.
\v 24 Nevertheless, Israel defeated them in battle and took possession of his land from the Arnon \add river\add* up as far as Yabok where the Ammonites border was well fortified.
\v 25 So Israel took those Amorite cities including Heshbon and the surrounding villages, and \add started\add* living in them.
\v 26 (Heshbon was where the Amorite King Sihon lived, as he’d \add previously\add* fought against the former king of Moab and captured all his land down as far as the Arnon \add river\add*.
\v 27 That was why the poets say,
\q1 “Come to Heshbon—let it be rebuilt.
\q1 Let \add King\add* Sihon’s city be established
\q1
\v 28 because fire went out from Heshbon,
\q1 flame from Sihon’s city.
\q1 It consumed Ar in Moab,
\q1 \add and\add* the citizens on Arnon’s hilltops.
\q1
\v 29 Moab, you won’t end well.
\q1 Chemosh’s people have perished.
\q1 He’s made his sons into fugitives,
\q1 and given his daughters over to captivity
\q1 by the Amorite King Sihon.
\q1
\v 30 We’ve overpowered them.
\q1 Heshbon has perished as far as Divon
\q1 and shattered Nofah as far as Meydeva.)
\p
\v 31 So the Israelites lived in the Amorite region
\v 32 and Moses sent \add some men\add* to spy out Yazer, and they captured its villages and drove the Amorites out from there.
\p
\v 33 Then they turned and went up the road \add towards\add* Bashan, but Bashan’s King Og came out with his army to meet them, and attacked them at Edre’i.
\v 34 Yahweh said to Moses, “Don’t be afraid of him because I’ve given him and all his people and his land to you, and you’ll \add be able to\add* do to him what you did to the Amorite King Sihon who lived at Heshbon.”
\v 35 So they overcame King Og and his sons and all his people until there were no remaining survivors, then they took possession of their land.
\c 22
\s1 King Balak sends for Balaam
\p
\v 1 Then the Israelites set off for the Moab plains, and they camped there across the river from Jericho.
\v 2 The \add Moabite King\add* Balak (Tsipor’s son), saw everything that Israel had done to the Amorites,
\v 3 and when he realised that the Israelites were very numerous, the Moabite \add people\add* became sick with fear.
\v 4 and they told the elders at Midian, “Now all that lot will devour everything that’s around us, \add just\add* like a cow completely cleans out a green field.”
\p Now Tsipor’s son Balak was Moab’s king at that time,
\v 5 and he sent messengers to Beor’s son Balaam at Petor (which was their tribal land near the \add Euphrates\add* river). He requested help, saying, “Listen, there’s a population here that came out from Egypt. Wow, they’ve covered the entire surface in this region and they’re living opposite me.
\v 6 So now please come and curse this people group for me because they are more numerous than us, \add then\add* perhaps I’ll be able to attack them and drive them out of the area, because I know that anyone you bless will be blessed, and those who you curse will be cursed.”
\p
\v 7 So the elders from Moab and from Midian departed carrying the fees for divination with them, and they went to Balaam and passed Balak’s words on to him.
\v 8 Balaam told them, “Stay here tonight, and \add in the morning\add*, I’ll bring you back answer, depending on what Yahweh tells me,” and \add so\add* the Moab leaders stayed \add there\add* with Balaam.
\p
\v 9 \add That night,\add* God came to Balaam and asked, “Who are those men with you?”
\p
\v 10 “Tsipor’s son, King Balak from Moab sent me a message,” Balaam replied.
\v 11 “\add He said that\add* shockingly a people group has come out of Egypt and has covered his region. \add He wants me to\add* go \add and\add* curse them for him, so that he’ll be able to attack them and drive them out of that area.”
\p
\v 12 Then God told Balaam, “Don’t go with them—you mustn’t curse those people, because they’re blessed.”
\v 13 The next morning, Balaam went over and told Balak’s messengers, “Go \add back\add* to your place, because Yahweh has refused to let me go with you.”
\v 14 So the Moab leaders stood up and went back to \add King\add* Balak, and told him, “Balaam refused to come with us.”
\p
\v 15 However, Balak sent \add back\add* a larger group of leaders who were even more senior than the first ones,
\v 16 and they went and told Balaam, “Tsipor’s son \add King\add* Balak says, ‘Please don’t refuse to come here,
\v 17 because I’ll honour you highly and do anything that you tell me to. Just please come and curse this population for me.’ ”
\p
\v 18 “\add Even\add* if Balak gave me a houseful of gold and sliver,” Balaam told Balak’s servant, “I \add still\add* couldn’t go against Yahweh’s instructions, even if it was just to do something small.
\v 19 However, please do stay here tonight, and I’ll find out if Yahweh might have anything else to say to me.”
\p
\v 20 That night, God came to Balaam and told him, “If those men have come to summon you, get ready and go with them, except that you must only give the message that I’ll tell you.”
\v 21 So in the morning, Balaam got ready and saddled his donkey, and went with the Moab leaders.
\s1 The donkey talks
\p
\v 22 However, God got angry because he’d gone, and Yahweh’s messenger stood in the middle of the road to block them. As Balaam rode on his donkey, accompanied by his two servants,
\v 23 the donkey saw Yahweh’s messenger standing there holding a sword in the middle of the road and turned off the road and went into a field. So Balaam whacked the donkey and forced it back onto the road.
\v 24 Then Yahweh’s messenger moved to a place where the road was very narrow, with vineyard walls on each side of the road.
\v 25 When the donkey saw Yahweh’s messenger, it squeezed close by the wall, and \add in doing so\add*, squashed Balaam’s foot against the wall, so he whacked it again.
\v 26 Then Yahweh’s messenger went past again and stood in a narrow place on the road where there was no \add room\add* to get past on either side.
\v 27 \add This time, when\add* the donkey saw the messenger, it lay down on the ground with Balaam sitting on top of it. Balaam got very angry and whacked the donkey \add again\add* with his staff.
\v 28 Then Yahweh enabled the donkey to speak, and it asked Balaam, “What did I do to you that you beat me these three times?”
\p
\v 29 “Because you’ve been messing me around!” Balaam shouted. “If only I’d been carrying a sword, then I would have killed you!”
\p
\v 30 “Aren’t I your donkey that you’ve ridden all your life?” the donkey asked Balaam. “Have I ever done anything like that to you before?”
\p And he said, “No.”
\p
\v 31 Then Yahweh uncovered Balaam’s eyes and he saw Yahweh’s messenger standing \add in the middle of\add* the road with his sword in his hand, and Balaam fell to his knees and kept bowing his face to the ground.
\v 32 Yahweh’s angel asked him, “Why did you strike you donkey those three times? Listen, I came personally to oppose you because you’re choosing a very dangerous path as far as I’m concerned.
\v 33 Your donkey saw me and avoided me those three times—if it hadn’t I certainly would have killed you already, but I would have let the donkey live.”
\p
\v 34 “\add Yes,\add* I’ve done wrong,” Balaam said to Yahweh’s messenger, “because I didn’t know that you were waiting for me \add there\add* on the road. But now, if you don’t want me to continue, I’ll return home again.”
\p
\v 35 “Continue on with those men,” Yahweh’s angel told Balaam, “but make sure that you only pass on the message that I give you.” So Balaam carried on with Balak’s leaders.
\s1 Balak welcomes Balaam
\p
\v 36 When \add King\add* Balak heard that Balaam was coming, he went out to meeting him at a city in Moab that was on his border beside the Arnon \add river\add*.
\v 37 He asked Balaam, “Didn’t I send an urgent message to summon you? Why didn’t you come \add then\add*? Did you think I couldn’t pay you enough?”
\p
\v 38 “Look, I’ve come \add now\add*,” Balaam answered Balak, “but can I say whatever I want? \add No,\add* I have to deliver whatever message God gives me.”
\v 39 So then Balaam went to Kiryat-Hutsot with Balak,
\v 40 where Balak sacrificed sheep and cattle, and gave \add some of the meat\add* to Balaam and to the leaders who were with them.
\v 41 The next morning, Balak took Balaam uphill to Bamot-Baal, and from there they could see the outskirts of the Israelite camp.
\c 23
\s1 Balaam blesses Israel
\p
\v 1 Then Balaam said to \add King\add* Balak, “\add Get them to\add* build seven altars for me here, and prepare me seven bulls and seven rams.”
\p
\v 2 So Balak did what Balaam had instructed, then the two of them offered up a bull and a ram on \add each\add* altar.
\v 3 Then Balaam told Balak, “You stand here beside your burnt offering, and I’ll move away. Perhaps Yahweh will want to meet me, and then I’ll tell you whatever he shows me.” Then he went to a bare hilltop
\v 4 where God met him, and Balaam told him, “I organised seven altars and offered up a bull and a ram on \add each\add* altar.”
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh gave Balaam a message and told him, “Go back to Balak and tell him that.”
\v 6 So he went back to Balak who was still standing by his burnt offering along with all of Moab’s officials.
\p
\v 7 So Balaam gave his pronouncement,
\q1 “\add It’s\add* Balak has brought me \add here\add* from Syria.
\q1 Moab’s king summoned me from the eastern mountains.
\q1 ‘Go curse Jacob for me,
\q1 and go denounce Israel.’
\q1
\v 8 How can I curse those who God hasn’t cursed?
\q1 How can I denounce those who Yahweh hasn’t denounced?
\q1
\v 9 Yes, I can see them from the top of \add those\add* rocks.
\q1 I look at them from the hills.
\q1 Look, a population that lives by itself.
\q1 It doesn’t count itself among the \add other\add* nations.
\q1
\v 10 Who can count the dust that Jacob \add leaves behind\add*?
\q1 Who can even count a quarter of the Israelites?
\q1 Let me die like \add those\add* honourable people,
\q1 and let my end be like theirs.”
\p
\v 11 But Balak demanded from Balaam, “What have you done to me? I brought you \add here\add* to curse my enemies, and blast it all—you’ve actually blessed them!”
\p
\v 12 “Shouldn’t I be careful to \add only\add* say what Yahweh tells me?” Balaam answered back.
\s1 Balaam’s second blessing
\p
\v 13 Then \add King\add* Balak told Balaam, “Please come with me to another place that you’ll be able to see them from. You won’t \add be able to\add* see all of them, only the edge \add of that large group\add*, but you’ll will curse Israel for me from there.”
\v 14 So he took Balaam to a field at the top of \add Mt.\add* Pisgah, and he built seven altars and offered up a bull and a ram on \add each\add* altar.
\p
\v 15 Balaam said to the king, “You stand here beside your burnt offering, while I go over there so \add Yahweh\add* can meet me.”
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh met with Balaam and gave him a message and told him, “Go back to Balak, and give him that message.”
\v 17 So he returned to him where he was standing with the leaders from Moab beside the altar, and Balak asked him, “What did Yahweh say?”
\p
\v 18 Then Balaam gave his speech,
\q1 “Look here, Balak, and listen.
\q1 Pay attention to me, son of Tsipor.
\q1
\v 19 God doesn’t lie because he’s not a human being,
\q1 and he’s not a mortal who might change his mind.
\q1 Whatever he’s said, he’ll do it.
\q1 Anything he promised, he’ll make it happen.
\q1
\v 20 Listen, I’ve received \add a command\add* to bless.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* he’s \add decided to\add* bless and I can’t reverse it.
\p
\v 21 Yahweh hasn’t considered hardship for Jacob,
\q1 and he hasn’t seen trouble for Israel.
\q1 His god Yahweh is with that people group,
\q1 and the king’s shout is over him.
\q1
\v 22 God brought them out of Egypt,
\q1 →\add giving them strength\add* like a wild bull’s horns.
\q1
\v 23 No, there’s no spell against Jacob,
\q1 and no divination against Israel.
\q1 \add People\add* will talk about Jacob/Israel,
\q1 →\add saying look\add* what God has done.
\q1
\v 24 That population stands up like a lioness,
\q1 and like a lion, it lifts itself up.
\q1 It won’t lie down until it’s devoured its prey
\q1 and drunk the blood of those it killed.”
\p
\v 25 Then \add King\add* Balak scolded Balaam, “Even if you won’t curse them, definitely don’t \em bless\em* them!”
\p
\v 26 “Didn’t I \add already\add* tell you,” Balaam answered Balak, “that I can only pronounce what Yahweh tells me to.”
\s1 Balaam’s third prophecy
\p
\v 27 Then \add King\add* Balak told Balaam, “Come with me. I’ll take you to another place. Perhaps it will pleased God if you curse them for me from there.”
\v 28 So Balak took him to the top of \add Mt.\add* Pe’or that faces down to the wilderness,
\v 29 and \add again\add* Balaam told him, “Build seven altars and prepare seven bulls and seven rams for me here.”
\v 30 So Balak followed those instructions and offered a bull and a ram on \add each\add* altar.
\c 24
\p
\v 1 By now, Balaam had realised that Yahweh wanted to bless the Israelites, so he didn’t use his usual divinations but instead, he just looked towards the wilderness.
\v 2 He looked up and saw Israel in their tents laid out by tribe. Then God’s spirit came onto him,
\v 3 and he made his pronouncement,
\q1 “\add This is\add* the declaration of Balaam, son of Beor—
\q1 the utterance of a man whose eyes are open.
\q1
\v 4 The declaration of someone who heard God’s message,
\q1 someone who sees the all-powerful one’s vision,
\q1 who bows to the ground with his eyes wide open.
\q1
\v 5 Yes Jacob, your tents look good.
\q1 Yes Israel, the places where you’re living:
\q1
\v 6 They stretch out like a riverbed.
\q1 Like gardens beside a river.
\q1 Like palms that Yahweh has planted,
\q1 Like cedar \add trees\add* beside the waters.
\q1
\v 7 Water will spill over the top of his buckets,
\q1 and his descendants will flow out like water.
\q1 Israel’s king will be greater than \add King\add* Agag,
\q1 and his kingdom will become honoured.
\q1
\v 8 God is bringing him \add here\add* from Egypt
\q1 \add with power\add* like the horns of a wild bull.
\q1 He’ll devastate those nations that oppose him.
\q1 He’ll break their bones, and his arrows will pierce them.
\q1
\v 9 He crouched—lying down like a lion or a lioness.
\q1 Who will rouse him?
\q1 Those who bless, you will be blessed,
\q1 ^but those who curse you, will be cursed.
\p
\v 10 Then \add King\add* Balak got very angry with Balaam, and he banged his hands together and said \add angrily\add*, “I called you to \em curse\em* my enemies, and blast you, you’ve actually blessed them three times \add now\add*!
\v 11 Now get back to where you came from! I said that I’d honour you very well, but man, Yahweh has kept you from any honour!”
\p
\v 12 But Balaam replied, “Didn’t I also tell the messengers that you sent to me that
\v 13 even if you gave me a house full of gold and silver, I wouldn’t be able to say anything other than what Yahweh tells me—whether good or evil? Whatever Yahweh tells me, that’s what I have to say.
\v 14 So yes, I’ll go back home now, but first, allow me to tell you what that people group will do to your people in the future.”
\p
\v 15 Then Balaam gave this pronouncement to Balak,
\q1 “\add This is\add* the declaration of Balaam, son of Beor—
\q1 the utterance of a man whose eyes are open.
\q1
\v 16 The statement from someone who hears God’s messages,
\q1 and the one who receives knowledge from the highest one,
\q1 who sees visions from the powerful one.
\q1 The one who falls to his knees with his eyes opened.
\q1
\v 17 I see him, but not now.
\q1 I’ll look at him, but he’s not close.
\q1 A star has marched out from Jacob,
\q1 and a staff has risen from Israel.
\q1 He will crush Moab’s corners,
\q1 and destroy all of Seth’s descendants.
\q1
\v 18 Edom will become their possession,
\q1 as will his enemy, Se’ir.
\q1 Israel will project strength.
\q1
\v 19 A ruler \add will come\add* from Jacob,
\q1 →and destroy the city’s survivors.”
\p
\v 20 Then Balaam looked towards Amalek and made this pronouncement,
\q1 “Amalek is the first of the nations,
\q2 ^but its end will be destruction.”
\p
\v 21 Then he looked towards the Keynim and made this pronouncement,
\q1 “Your residence is permanent,
\q1 and your nest is set in the cliffs.
\q1
\v 22 but you Keynim will be destroyed
\q1 when Assyria \add the Assyrians)\add* take you captive.”
\p
\v 23 Then \add finally,\add* Balaam made this pronouncement,
\q1 “Oh dear, who can survive when God does \add all\add* that?
\q1
\v 24 Ships will come from the Kitim coast,
\q1 and they’ll damage Assyria and Eber,
\q1 but they themselves will be destroyed as well.”
\p
\v 25 Then Balaam packed up and went home, and \add King\add* Balak also went on his way.
\c 25
\s1 Israel worships Baal at Pe’or
\p
\v 1 Meanwhile, the Israelites settled down at Shitim, and their men started sleeping with women from Moab
\v 2 who then invited them to sacrifices to their idols. So the Israelites bowed down \add to the idols\add* and ate \add at the feasts\add*,
\v 3 and \add when\add* Israel consecrated themselves to Baal-Pe’or, Yahweh’s anger burnt towards them.
\v 4 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Execute all the people’s leaders and display their bodies in broad daylight, and then Yahweh’s anger might turn back away from Israel.”
\p
\v 5 So Moses to Israel’s judges, “Each of you must execute your men who have joined others to serve Baal-Pe’or.”
\p
\v 6 Just then, unbelievably, an Israelite man came past Moses and all the people who were \add there outside\add* the sacred tent weeping, and he was bringing a Midianite woman with him.
\v 7 When Pinhas (son of Eleazar, son of Aaron saw that, he came out from among the gathered people, grabbed a spear,
\v 8 and followed the Israelite man into his tent. He plunged the spear through the two of them—right through the man and the woman’s belly, and \add thus\add*, stopped the deadly disease \add that had started\add* against the Israelites.
\v 9 However, twenty-four thousand people had \add already\add* died from that plague.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh spoke to Moses,
\v 11 “Aaron’s grandson Pinhas has deflected my anger from the Israelites by demonstrating my seriousness about their behaviour, so now in my zeal, I won’t eliminate the Israelites.
\v 12 So tell him that I’m making a peace agreement with him.
\v 13 It’s an agreement that gives him and his descendants the rights to be priests because he was zealous on his god’s behalf and he worked towards removing the guilt of the people.”
\p
\v 14 The Israelite man who was killed with the Midianite woman was Salu’s son Zimri, a clan leader from the Simeon tribe.
\v 15 The Midianite woman who was killed was Kozbi, the daughter of Tsur, a Midianite clan leader.
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 17 “Attack the Midianites and destroy them
\v 18 because they were treacherously deceiving you all over the matter of Pe’or and Kozbi, the daughter of one of their leaders who was killed on the day of the deadly disease at \add Mt.\add* Pe’or.”
\c 26
\s1 The second census
\p
\v 1 After \add the incident of\add* the deadly disease, Yahweh told Moses and Aaron’s son Eleazar the priest,
\v 2 “Count all the Israelite people \add again\add* listing all those by their clans who are twenty and over, and capable of going out to battle for Israel.”
\v 3 So there in the Moab plains, across the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho, Moses and the priest Eleazar gave these instructions to the people,
\v 4 “Yahweh has ordered us to list the names of the \add men\add* who came out from Egypt who are twenty years old or older.
\s1 Reuben’s descendants
\p
\v 5 Reuben was Israel’s eldest son, and his descendants came via his sons Hanok, Palu,
\v 6 Hezron, and Karmi.
\v 7 Those clans added up to 43,730 men.
\v 8 Palu’s son was Eliav,
\v 9 and Eliav’s son were Nemuel, Datan, and Aviram. (That was the Datan and Aviram who were the leaders that rebelled against Moses and Aaron when they joined Korah’s rebellion against Yahweh.
\v 10 Then the earth split open and swallowed them and Korah. Yahweh had also sent a fire that consumed 250 men, and they became a warning sign.
\v 11 However, Korah’s descendants hadn’t died \add then\add*.)
\s1 Simeon’s descendants
\p
\v 12 Simeon’s descendants came via his sons Nemuel, Yamin, Yakin,
\v 13 Zerah, and Shaul.
\v 14 Those clans added up to 22,200 men.
\s1 Gad’s descendants
\p
\v 15 Gad’s descendants came via his sons Tsefon, Hagit, Shuni,
\v 16 Ozni, Eir,
\v 17 Arod, and Areli.
\v 18 Those clans added up to 40,500 men.
\s1 Judah’s descendants
\p
\v 19 Judah had two sons Er and Onan who died \add childless\add* in Canaan.
\v 20 His descendants came via his \add other\add* sons Shelah, Perez, and Zerah.
\v 21 Perez’s descendants \add came via his sons\add* Hezron and Hamul.
\v 22 Those clans added up to 76,500 men.
\s1 Issachar’s descendants
\p
\v 23 Issachar’s descendants came via his sons Tola, Puah,
\v 24 Yashuv, and Shimron.
\v 25 Those clans added up to 64,300 men.
\s1 Zebulun’s descendants
\p
\v 26 Zebulun’s descendants came via his sons Sered, Eylon, and Yahle’el.
\v 27 Those clans added up to 60,500 men.
\s1 Manasseh’s descendants
\p
\v 28 Joseph’s descendants came via his sons Manasseh and Ephraim.
\v 29 Manasseh’s descendants came from his son Makir and then his son Gilead.
\v 30 Gilead’s descendants came via his sons I’ezer, Helek,
\v 31 Asriel, Shechem,
\v 32 Shemida, and Hefer.
\v 33 Hefer’s son Tselofehad had no sons—only daughters named Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milkah, and Tirtsah.
\v 34 Those clans added up to 52,700 men.
\s1 Ephraim’s descendants
\p
\v 35 Ephraim’s descendants came via his sons Shutelah, Beker, and Tahan,
\v 36 Shutelah’s descendants came via his son Eran.
\v 37 Those clans added up to 32,500 men. Those last two totals were \add all\add* Joseph’s descendants.
\s1 Benjamin’s descendants
\p
\v 38 Benjamin’s descendants came via his sons Bela, Ashbel, Ahiram,
\v 39 Shefufam, and Hufam.
\v 40 Bela’s descendants came via his sons Ard and Naaman.
\v 41 Those clans added up to 45,600 men.
\s1 Dan’s descendants
\p
\v 42 Dan’s descendants came via his son Shuham.
\v 43 That clan had 64,400 men.
\s1 Asher’s descendants
\p
\v 44 Asher’s descendants belonged to Yimnah’s clan, Yishvi’s clan, and Beriah’s clan.
\v 45 Beriah’s descendants came via his sons Hever and Malkiel.
\v 46 Asher’s daughter was named Serah.
\v 47 Those clans added up to 53,400 men.
\s1 Naphtali’s descendants
\p
\v 48 Naphtali’s descendants came via his sons Yahtse’el, Guni,
\v 49 Yetser, and Shilem.
\v 50 Those clans added up to 45,400 men.
\s1 The census total and the division of land
\p
\v 51 The total of all those tribes counted came to 601,730 men.\f + \fr 26:51 \ft Slightly smaller than the total of 603,550 from the first census, see \xt 1:46\ft .\f*
\p
\v 52 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 53 “The land should be allocated to those \add clans\add* according to the number of their names, to become their inheritance.
\v 54 Those with larger numbers should receive more, and the smaller groups receive less, \add i.e.,\add* each inheritance depends on the number of family members.
\v 55 You must throw lots to determine which clan gets which piece of land. The lands of the clans must be within their tribal lands.
\v 56 Then the size of the area that will become their inheritance must be apportioned according to the census numbers.
\s1 Levi’s descendants
\p
\v 57 The listed Levites included the clans \add from Levi’s sons\add* Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari,
\v 58 which were the Livnim, the Hevronim, the Mahlim, the Mushim, and the Korhim. Also, Korah fathered Amram
\v 59 whose wife was Levi’s daughter Yokeved who’d been born in Egypt. They had two sons Aaron and Moses, and their \add older\add* sister Miriam.
\v 60 Aaron’s sons were Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar,
\v 61 but Nadav and Avihu had died when they’d offered Yahweh fire in a disobedient manner.
\v 62 The male \add Levites\add* who were a month old or older came to 23,000, but they weren’t included in the above nation-wide count because they didn’t inherit land in the same way as other tribes.
\s1 Census summary
\p
\v 63 Those were the census results from when Moses and the priest Eleazar counted the Israelites on the Moab plains on the east side of the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho.
\v 64 None of the men on those lists were on the previous lists done by Moses and the priest Aaron when they’d counted the Israelites in the Sinai wilderness,
\v 65 because Yahweh had told them that they’d certainly die \add there\add* in the wilderness until none of those alive were left other than Yefuneh’s son Caleb and Nun’s son Joshua.
\c 27
\s1 Inheritances without sons
\p
\v 1 Then the daughters of Tselofehad (son of Hefer, son of Gilead, son of Makir, son of Manasseh approached. Their names were Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milkah, and Tirtsah.
\v 2 They stood in front of Moses and the priest Eleazar and all the leaders and the people \add there\add* at the entrance to the sacred tent, \add and\add* asked,
\v 3 “Our father died in the wilderness, although he wasn’t part of the group who joined Korah to rebel against Yahweh—it was for his own personal disobedience that he died. He didn’t have any sons
\v 4 \add but\add* why should his family name disappear from our clan \add just\add* because he had no sons? \add So\add* give us some land like our father’s relatives will be getting.”
\p
\v 5 So Moses took their case to Yahweh
\v 6 and he responded,
\v 7 “It’s right what Tselofehad’s daughters are saying. You must give them some land like their father’s relatives will be getting. Give them whatever would have been given to their father as his inheritance.
\v 8 Also, tell the people that if a man dies without sons, then his inheritance should go to his daughter(s).
\v 9 If he had no children, then it should go to his brothers,
\v 10 and if he has no brothers, then it should go to his father’s brothers.
\v 11 However, if his father had no brothers, then the inheritance should go to his nearest blood-relative to become his possession. That will become a permanent regulation for Israel, just as Yahweh has commanded.”
\s1 Joshua succeeds Moses
\r (Deu. 31:1-8)
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Go up Mt. Avarim and look at the land that I’ve given to the Israelites,
\v 13 and then you’ll die and join your ancestors just like your brother Aaron did.
\v 14 That’s because back in the Zin wilderness when the people complained about water, you two rebelled against my command and failed to respect my holiness in front of them. That was \add when you struck the rock\add* at Merivah \add near\add* Kadesh in the Zin wilderness.”
\p
\v 15 Then Moses requested Yahweh,
\v 16 “Yahweh, \add you\add* are the god who directs the spirits of all humankind, \add so please\add* appoint a new leader for the Israelite people
\v 17 who will stand in front of them and lead them out and bring them in so they won’t be like sheep without a shepherd.
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh told Moses, “Get Nun’s son Joshua, a man who has my spirit in him, and place your hands on him \add to indicate the transfer of leadership\add*.
\v 19 Stand him in front of the priest Eleazar and all the assembly, and commission him as they watch.
\v 20 Give him some of your authority so that all the Israelite people can hear it.
\v 21 When \add he needs guidance\add*, he must stand in front of the priest Eleazar and ask for Yahweh’s decision \add to be given via\add* the Urim. Then Joshua and all the people will go in or come out as per Yahweh’s instructions.”
\v 22 So Moses did what Yahweh had ordered him to, and he presented Joshua to the priest Eleazar and to the entire community.
\v 23 Moses placed his hands on Joshua and commissioned him, just has Yahweh had said he should.
\c 28
\s1 The daily offerings
\r (Exo. 29:38-46)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Give the Israelites these instructions: Ensure that you present to me the offerings \add and\add* food for offerings to be burnt at the proper time.
\v 3 For the daily burnt offering, present two one-year-old, male lambs that have no defects.
\v 4 One of those lambs must be offered in the morning, and the other in late afternoon,
\v 5 \add each offered\add* along with a kilogram of fine flour mixed with a litre of \add olive\add* oil.
\v 6 That’s the regular burnt offering done like the first one at Mt. Sinai—the soothing aroma from the fire will be pleasing to Yahweh.
\v 7 The drink offering must \add also\add* be one litre for each lamb. That strong drink must be poured out in the sacred area as an offering to Yahweh.
\v 8 At the end of the afternoon, offer the second lamb with the same-sized offerings of flour and wine as in the morning. When they’re burnt, the aroma will be pleasing to Yahweh.
\s1 The Rest Day offerings
\p
\v 9 “On the Rest Day, \add it must be\add* two one-year-old, male lambs with no defects, along with two kilograms of fine flour mixed with oil, as well as the drink offering.
\v 10 That will be burnt every Rest Day, in addition to the regular \add daily\add* burnt offering and its drink offering.
\s1 The new month offerings
\p
\v 11 “On the first day of each month, you all must bring Yahweh a burnt offering of two young bulls, one ram, and seven one-year-old, male lambs with no defects.
\v 12 Each bull must be offered with three kilograms of fine flour mixed with oil, each ram with two kilograms,
\v 13 and each lamb with one kilogram. \add All of that\add* will be a burnt offering with an aroma that will be pleasing to Yahweh.
\v 14 Their \add respective\add* drink offerings will be two litres of wine with the bull, 1.3 litres with the ram, and one litre with each lamb. That will be the burnt offering for the start of each new month throughout the year.
\v 15 In addition to those burnt offerings and drink offerings, a male goat must \add also\add* be presented to Yahweh as a sin offering.
\s1 The ‘pass-over’ offerings
\r (Lev. 23:5-14)
\p
\v 16 “Yahweh’s ‘passover’ \add ceremony will start\add* on the 14th of the first month \add (Nisan = March/April) each year\add*.
\v 17 The 15th will be \add the start of\add* the celebration—all your bread must be made without any rising agent.
\v 18 That first day, there’s to be a sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day.
\v 19 You all must offer two young bulls, one ram, and seven one-year-old lambs with no defects to be burnt on the fire.
\v 20 Their \add associated\add* grain offerings must be three kilograms of fine flour mixed with \add olive\add* for the bull, two kilograms for the ram,
\v 21 \add and\add* one kilogram for each of the seven lambs.
\v 22 Also, bring one male goat as a sin offering to make yourselves right with God.
\v 23 Those offerings must be \em in addition\em* to the regular morning offerings
\v 24 \add and\add* must be offered for each of the seven days. The aroma of those burnt offerings will be pleasing to Yahweh, along with the regular burnt offering and its drink offering.
\v 25 On the seventh day, there’s to be \add another\add* sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day.
\s1 Harvest festival offerings
\r (Lev. 23:15-22)
\p
\v 26 “On the firstfruits day of the harvest festival, when you all present an offering of fresh grain to Yahweh, there’s to be a sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day.
\v 27 You all must present a burnt offering that’s a pleasing aroma for Yahweh, \add presenting\add* two \add young\add* bulls, one ram, and seven one-year-old male lambs.
\v 28 Their \add associated\add* grain offerings will be three kilograms of fine flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil for each bull, two kilograms for the ram,
\v 29 and one kilogram for each of the lambs.
\v 30 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God.
\v 31 Those offerings along with their drink offerings must be offered in addition to the regular burnt offering and drink offerings. Ensure that they have no defects.
\c 29
\s1 The trumpeting celebration
\r (Lev. 23:23-25)
\p
\v 1 “On the first day of the seventh month \add (Tishrei = September/October) each year\add*, there’s to be a sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day—it’ll be a day of blowing horns.
\v 2 You all must present a young bull, a ram, and seven one-year-old lambs, \add all\add* with no defects as a burnt offering to be a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 3 Their \add associated\add* grain offering will be three kilograms of fine flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil with the bull, two kilograms with the ram,
\v 4 and one kilogram for each of the seven lambs.
\v 5 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God.
\v 6 Those are in addition to the monthly and regular \add daily\add* burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain offerings and drink offerings. The aroma of those burnt offerings will be pleasing to Yahweh.
\s1 The sacrifice for forgiveness
\r (Lev. 23:26-32)
\p
\v 7 “Each year on the 10th of this seventh month \add (Tishrei = September/October)\add*, there’s to be a sacred assembly. Don’t eat any food or do any regular work on that day.
\v 8 You all must present a young bull, a ram, and seven one-year-old male lambs as a burnt offering that will be a pleasing aroma for Yahweh. Ensure that they have no defects.
\v 9 Their \add associated\add* grain offerings will be three kilograms of fine flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil for the bull, two kilograms for the ram,
\v 10 and one kilogram for each of the seven lambs.
\v 11 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat, in addition to the one to make yourselves right with God and the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\s1 Offerings during the celebration in shelters
\r (Lev. 23:33-44)
\p
\v 12 “On the 15th of that seventh month, there’s to be \add another\add* sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day \add which will begin\add* seven days of celebration.
\v 13 “You all must present thirteen young bulls, two rams, fourteen one-year-old male lambs as a burnt offering that will be a pleasing aroma for Yahweh. Ensure that they have no defects.
\v 14 Their \add associated\add* grain offerings will be three kilograms of fine flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil for each bull, two kilograms for each ram,
\v 15 and one kilogram for each of the seven lambs.
\v 16 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 17 “On the second day, you all must offer twelve young bulls, two rams, fourteen one-year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 18 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 19 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 20 “On the third day, \add the sacrifice will be\add* eleven young bulls, two rams, fourteen year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 21 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 22 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 23 “On the fourth day, \add the sacrifice will be\add* ten young bulls, two rams, fourteen year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 24 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 25 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 26 “On the fifth day, \add the sacrifice will be\add* nine young bulls, two rams, fourteen year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 27 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 28 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 29 “On the sixth day, \add the sacrifice will be\add* eight young bulls, two rams, fourteen year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 30 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 31 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 32 “On the seventh day, \add the sacrifice will be\add* seven young bulls, two rams, fourteen year-old male lambs without defects,
\v 33 and their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings as already stipulated.
\v 34 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 35 “On the eighth \add and final\add* day, there’s to be \add another\add* sacred assembly. No regular work may be done that day.
\v 36 You all must present a young bull, a ram, and seven one-year-old male lambs, \add all\add* unblemished, as a burnt offering that will be a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 37 Their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings must be as already stipulated.
\v 38 \add Also\add* for your sins, sacrifice one male goat to make yourselves right with God, in addition to the regular burnt offerings with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings.
\p
\v 39 “\add The above are the regulations for what\add* you all must do for Yahweh at the appointed times, in addition to your vows and voluntary offerings, burnt offerings, grain and drink offerings, and your peace offerings.”
\p
\v 40 So Moses passed on to the Israelites everything that Yahweh had instructed him.
\c 30
\s1 Instructions about vows
\p
\v 1 Then Moses gave the Israelite leaders these further instructions from Yahweh,
\v 2 “If a man makes a solemn promise to Yahweh, or makes a binding vow on his own life, he mustn’t dishonour what he said—he must follow whatever came out of his mouth.
\p
\v 3 If a young woman who is still living with her parents solemnly promises to Yahweh to do something,
\v 4 and if her father hears her make the binding vow on her life and he remains silent, then her vow and all her obligations will stand.
\v 5 However, if her father forbids her when he hears her, then that vow and its obligations won’t stand, and Yahweh will forgive her, because her father stepped in.
\p
\v 6 If she gets engaged or married while a vow or thoughtless promise still holds,
\v 7 and her husband hears about it and remains silent that day, then her vow and or thoughtless promise will stand.
\v 8 However, if her husband forbids her when he hears her, then that vow and its obligations won’t stand, and Yahweh will forgive her.
\p
\v 9 If a widow or a woman who has been divorced makes a promise, she must do what she promised.
\p
\v 10 If a married woman makes a solemn promise, or makes a binding vow on her own life,
\v 11 and her husband hears about it but says nothing and doesn’t forbid her, then her vow and all her obligations will stand.
\v 12 However, if he actually nullifies it when he hears about it, any promise that she pronounced or binding vow on her life, won’t stand. Her husband has nullified them and Yahweh will forgive her,
\v 13 \add so\add* every vow and its obligations can either be nullified or confirmed by her husband.
\v 14 If her husband has stayed silent for several days, that will confirm all her vows and/or obligations because he said nothing.
\v 15 However, if he cancels them \add sometime\add* after he’d heard about them, then he will bear her guilt.
\p
\v 16 Those are the instructions that Yahweh gave Moses \add about promises\add* between a man and his wife, \add and\add* between a father and his unmarried daughter living at home.
\c 31
\s1 Avenging the Midianites
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Avenge the Midianites for what they did to \add you\add* Israelites. After that, you’ll go to join your ancestors.”
\p
\v 3 So Moses told the people, “Equip some of your men to battle against Midian to execute Yahweh’s vengeance on them—
\v 4 one thousand men from each tribe to go to war.”
\p
\v 5 So a thousand men were conscripted from each tribe—twelve thousand equipped for battle
\v 6 and Moses sent them off to war. The priest Pinhas (Eleazar’s son) went with them, taking some sacred objects as well as the signal trumpets.
\v 7 Then they fought Midian just as Yahweh had commanded Moses, and they killed every male.
\v 8 \add Among them,\add* they killed five Midian kings: Evi, Rekem, Tsur, Hur, and Reva, in addition to Beor’s son Balaam.
\p
\v 9 The Israelites took the Midian women and children captive, and took all their cattle, sheep and goats, and all their possessions as plunder,
\v 10 then they burnt down all their cities and houses and camps.
\v 11 They took all the spoil and plunder including the people and animals back,
\v 12 and brought it to Moses and the priest Eleazar, and to the assembled Israelites who were \add all still\add* camped on the Moab plains beside the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho.
\s1 Purifying the returning warriors
\p
\v 13 Moses and the priest Eleazar and all the Israelite leaders left the camp to meet them,
\v 14 but Moses was angry at the top army leaders who were coming back from the battle.
\v 15 “Why did you allow all the women to live?” he asked them.
\v 16 “They’re the very ones who did what Balaam suggested to urge the people to be unfaithful to Yahweh at Pe’or, leading to the deadly disease \add that struck\add* Yahweh’s people.
\v 17 So now, kill all the boys plus every woman who’s slept with a man—
\v 18 spare only the young, unmarried girls for yourselves.
\p
\v 19 “Then everyone who’s killed someone or touched a dead body must stay outside the camp for seven days. On the third day, you all must purify yourselves, and \add again\add* on the seventh day \add but this time\add* including your captives.
\v 20 \add Also,\add* you all must purify all your clothes, plus anything \add else\add* made of leather or goats’ hair or wood.”
\p
\v 21 Then the priest Eleazar told the warriors who’d gone to battle, “This is what Yahweh has instructed Moses:
\v 22 Only the gold, silver, bronze, iron, tin, and lead,
\v 23 must pass through fire to make it ‘clean’. Nevertheless, it must still be purified with the \add special\add* purifying water, along with anything that wouldn’t handle the fire.
\v 24 On the seventh day, you all must wash your clothes and then you’ll be ‘clean’ and be allowed to reenter the camp.
\s1 Dividing the plunder
\p
\v 25 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 26 “You and the priest Eleazar and the tribal leaders must list all the plunder including both human and animals that were taken captive.
\v 27 Then you must divide it all out \add equally between the two groups\add*: those who went out to battle, and all \add the rest of\add* the community.
\v 28 Set aside a tribute for Yahweh from \add the plunder of\add* the warriors who fought in the battle: one from every five-hundred \add captive\add* people, and the same from the cattle and donkeys, and from the sheep and goats.
\v 29 Half of that must be given to the priest Eleazar as the contribution to Yahweh.
\v 30 Then from the half \add of the plunder\add* \add given\add* to the community, take one from every fifty \add captive\add* people, and the same from the cattle and donkeys, and from the sheep and goats and other animals. That’s to be given to the Levites because they’re the ones who look after Yahweh’s residence.”
\v 31 So Moses and the priest Eleazar did what Yahweh had told them to do.
\p
\v 32 The remaining plunder \add after the slaughter of the boys and the married women\add* from what the warriors had brought back was: 675,00 sheep,
\v 33 72,000 cattle,
\v 34 61,000 donkeys,
\v 35 and 32,000 girls who hadn’t been married.
\v 36 The warriors who’d fought got: 337,500 sheep,
\v 37 less 675 that were given to Yahweh \add as his tribute\add*.
\v 38 The cattle were 36,000 less 72 for Yahweh.
\v 39 There were 30,500 donkeys less Yahweh’s tribute of sixty-one.
\v 40 The girls and young women were 16,000 less 32 for Yahweh.
\v 41 So Moses gave Yahweh’s contribution to the priest Eleazar as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 42 The half for the community that Moses had divided off from the warriors
\v 43 was: 337,500 sheep,
\v 44 36,000 cattle,
\v 45 30,500 donkeys,
\v 46 and 16,000 girls who hadn’t been married.
\v 47 From that half for the community, Moses took one from every fifty people and animals, and gave it to the Levites who serve in Yahweh’s tent, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 48 Then the top army officers (the commanders over thousands, and the commanders over hundreds), went to Moses
\v 49 and informed him, “\add We,\add* your servants have counted the soldiers who were under us, and none of our men are missing,
\v 50 so we’ve brought an offering to Yahweh: every man who found something gold \add like\add* an armlet or bracelets, signet ring or earring, or necklaces \add has donated it\add* to make us right with Yahweh.”
\v 51 So Moses and the priest Eleazar took those crafted gold items from them,
\v 52 and the total contribution of gold offered to Yahweh by the commanders of thousands and of hundreds weighed around 190 kilograms.
\v 53 Each of the warriors had taken their own plunder,
\v 54 and Moses and the priest Eleazar took the gold from the commanders of thousands and of hundreds, and brought it to the sacred tent as a memorial for the Israelites in front of Yahweh.
\c 32
\s1 The tribes who stayed east of Jordan
\r (Deu. 3:12-22)
\p
\v 1 The \add people\add* from the Reuben and Gad tribes \add had amassed\add* a lot of livestock, and they noticed that the Yazer and Gilead regions were very suitable for livestock.
\v 2 So their leaders went to Moses and the priest Eleazar and the community leaders to say,
\v 3 \add The regions of\add* Atarot, Divon, Yazer, Nimrah, Heshbon, El’aleh, Sevam, Nevo, and Be’on
\v 4 are \add in\add* the land \add here\add* that Yahweh has handed over to us Israelites. It’s \add ideal\add* for livestock, and \add we,\add* your servants, raise livestock.
\v 5 If we’ve earnt your favour, let this land be given to your servants for us to own, \add rather than forcing us\add* to cross over the Jordan \add river with all the other tribes\add*.”
\p
\v 6 “It wouldn’t be right for your fellow Israelites to go to war while you all \add just\add* stay here,” Moses told them.
\v 7 “So why do you discourage Israelite enthusiasm for crossing over into the land that Yahweh has given them?
\v 8 Your parents did that at Kadesh-Barnea when I sent them \add in\add* to survey the land.
\v 9 They went as far as the Eshkol valley and they saw the land, yet they discouraged the people from entering the land that Yahweh had given them.
\v 10 Yahweh got very angry that day, and promised,
\v 11 ‘From the men who came out of Egypt, no man who’s twenty or older will see the land which I promised to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, because they’re not fully with me.
\v 12 Only Yefuneh’s son Caleb and Nun’s son Joshua will enter because they have believed me.’
\v 13 So Yahweh got angry with Israel, and made us wander in the wilderness for forty years until that generation was eliminated who had been evil as per Yahweh’s judgement.
\v 14 Now look here! You’re standing in front of me \add just\add* like your parents—a brood of disobedient people who’ll make Yahweh more angry with you than he was with your parents!
\v 15 If you all turn away from following him, he’ll abandon you all in the wilderness again, and you will have destroyed this nation.”
\p
\v 16 However, they stepped closer to him and said, “\add First\add* we’ll build pens for our animals, and build cities for our families \add here\add*.
\v 17 Then we’ll equip ourselves for war, to go ahead of the rest of Israel until we’ve brought them into their place. Meanwhile our families will live in those fortified cities because there’ll still be threats from those who live in this region.
\v 18 We won’t return to our homes until every Israelite is in possession of his inheritance.
\v 19 We won’t take any land \add there\add* on the other side of the Jordan \add river\add* or beyond because this will be our inheritance here on the eastern side.”
\p
\v 20 “If you’re going to do that,” Moses responded, “then you all must equip yourselves to fight battles for Yahweh,
\v 21 and all of your armed warriors must cross the Jordan to serve Yahweh until he’s driven his enemies out \add ahead of him\add*.
\v 22 \add Once\add* the land is subdued before Yahweh, then you all can return. You’ll be free of obligation from Yahweh and from Israel, and this land \add here\add* will be your possession from Yahweh.
\v 23 However, if you all don’t help the rest of us, you will be disobeying Yahweh, and your disobedience will catch up with you.
\v 24 \add So go ahead and\add* build your cities for your families, and pens for your flocks, and do what you’ve committed to.”
\p
\v 25 “Your servants will do what you’ve said,” confirmed the \add leaders of\add* the Reuben and Gad tribes.
\v 26 “Our wives and children, and our livestock and all our animals will remain in cities here in the Gilead \add region\add*,
\v 27 but we’ll equip ourselves for battle and to cross the river to battle for Yahweh just as my master has spoken.”
\p
\v 28 So Moses gave instructions about them to the priest Eleazar, Nun’s son Joshua, and the Israelite tribal leaders,
\v 29 telling them, “If the men from Gad and Reuben tribes cross over the Jordan with you and equipped ready to fight for Yahweh and that land is conquered, then you must give them the Gilead region for them to own.
\v 30 However, if they don’t cross over with \add the rest of\add* you, then they’ll have to accept land to own \add there\add* in Canaan.”
\p
\v 31 “What Yahweh has told you, his servants, that’s what we’ll do,” the leaders of the Gad and Reuben tribes responded.
\v 32 We’ll send armed men across to Canaan, equipped to fight for Yahweh. However, our inheritance to own will be \add here\add* on this side of the Jordan \add river\add*.”
\p
\v 33 So Moses gave that region to the Reuben and Gad tribes, the half-tribe of Joseph’s son Manasseh, \add encompassing\add* the kingdoms of the Amorite King Sihon and Bashan’s King Og, plus the surrounding territories.
\v 34 The Gadim rebuilt Divon, Atarot, Aroer,
\v 35 Atrot-Shofan, Yazer, Yagebehah,
\v 36 Beyt-Nimrah, and Beyt-Haran—\add all\add* fortified cities with pens for their flocks.
\v 37 The Re’uvenim rebuilt Heshbon, El’aleh, Kiryatayim,
\v 38 Nevo, Baal-Me’on, and Sivmah, but they renamed \add some\add* of the cities.
\p
\v 39 The descendants of Manasseh’s son Makir went to Gilead and captured it—driving out the Amorites who lived there.
\v 40 So Moses gave Gilead to Manasseh’s son Makir, and \add his clan\add* lived there.
\v 41 Manasseh’s \add other\add* son Yair went and captured the Amorite villages and named them ‘Yair’s Towns’ \add (or ‘Havot-Ya’ir’)\add*.
\v 42 \add A man named\add* Novah went and captured Kenat and its villages, and renamed it to ‘Novah’ after himself.
\c 33
\s1 The journey legs from Egypt to Sinai
\p
\v 1 The following are \add the details\add* of the stages of Israel’s journeys \add starting from\add* when they exited Egypt tribe-by-tribe under Moses and Aaron’s leadership.
\v 2 Moses, acting on Yahweh’s instructions, had written down their departure points, so these were \add the legs of\add* their journeys:
\p
\v 3 The day after \add they celebrated\add* the ‘pass-over’ (around the end of March), they left Ra’meses \add city\add*, raising one fist in full view of all Egypt.
\v 4 Meanwhile, the Egyptians were burying all their oldest sons who’d been struck down by Yahweh who had \add also\add* totally humiliated their gods.
\p
\v 5 After leaving Ra’meses, the Israelites \add went\add* to Sukot and camped \add there\add*.
\v 6 Then they left Sukot and camped at Eytam at the edge of the wilderness.
\v 7 After Eytam, they turned back to Pi-Hahirot (opposite Baal-Tsefon) and camped facing Migdal.
\v 8 Then they left Pi-Hahirot and walked through the middle of the sea into the Eytam wilderness and camped at Marah.
\v 9 From Marah, they went to Eylim which had twelve springs with seventy palm trees \add around them\add*, and they camped there.
\v 10 After Eylam, they camped beside the Red sea
\v 11 before leaving the sea and camping in the Sin wilderness.\f + \fr 33:11 \ft Note that this is located in the southwestern Sinai peninsula (between Eylim and Mt. Sinai), and different from the Zin \+add (Zin)\+add* wilderness (which is located in the northern Negev desert, near Kadesh-Barnea).\f*
\v 12 Travelling from there, they camped at Dofkah,
\v 13 and then from there, at Alush.
\v 14 After leaving Alush, they camped at Refidim \add but\add* they didn’t have water there to drink.
\v 15 When they left Refidim, they camped in the Sinai wilderness.
\s1 The journey legs in the wilderness
\p
\v 16 From the Sinai wilderness, they went to Kivrot-Hata’avah and camped \add there\add*.
\v 17 When they left Kivrot-Hata’avah, they camped at Hatserot,
\v 18 and then from there, at Ritmah.
\v 19 From Ritmah, they \add went to\add* Rimon-Perez to camp,
\v 20 and after that, on to Livnah.
\v 21 Then they left Livnah and camped at Risah,
\v 22 before moving on from there to camp at Kehelatah.
\v 23 From Kehelatah, they camped at Mt. Shefer,
\v 24 then they left the mountain and camped at Haradah.
\v 25 When they left Haradah, they camped at Makhelot,
\v 26 and after that, on to Tahat.
\v 27 From Tahat, they camped at Terah,
\v 28 and then from Terah, they went to Mitkah to camp.
\v 29 After Mitkah, they camped at Hashmonah,
\v 30 and next after that, they camped at Moserot.
\v 31 Then they left Moserot and camped at Beney-Ya’akan.
\v 32 From Beney-Ya’akan, they \add went to\add* Hor-Hagidgad and camped \add there\add*,
\v 33 then they left and camped at Yotvatah.
\v 34 Then they left Yotvatah and camped at Avronah.
\v 35 From Avronah, they \add went to\add* Etsyon-Gever and camped \add there\add*,
\v 36 then they left and camped in the Zin wilderness, i.e., at Kadesh.
\v 37 Then they left Kadesh and camped at Mt. Hor by the border with Edom.
\p
\v 38 Aaron the priest climbed Mt. Hor following Yahweh’s instructions, and then died there on the 5th of the first month, forty years after the Israelites had left Egypt.
\v 39 He was 123 years old when he died \add there\add* on Mt. Hor.
\p
\v 40 Meanwhile, the Canaanite king of Arad who lived in the Negev in Canaan, heard that the Israelites were coming.
\p
\v 41 The Israelites left Mt. Hor and camped at Tsalmonah.
\v 42 When they left Tsalmonah, they camped at Punon,
\v 43 and after that, on to Ovot.
\v 44 From Ovot, they \add went on\add* to Iyey-Ha’avarim at the Moab border.
\v 45 Then they left there and camped at Divon-Gad,
\v 46 before going on to camp at Almon-Divlatayim.
\v 47 From there, they \add went into\add* the Avarim mountains and camped facing Nevo.
\v 48 After leaving the Avarim mountains, they reached the Moab plains by the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho.
\v 49 They camped beside the river from Beyt-Hayeshimot to Abel-Hashitim \add there\add* on the Moab plains.
\p
\v 50 Then Yahweh spoke to Moses \add there\add* on the Moab plains beside the Jordan opposite Jericho,
\v 51 “Tell the Israelites that you’re all about to cross the Yahweh to enter Canaan.
\v 52 Then you all must drive all the land’s inhabitants out ahead of you, and destroy all their carved images and cast metal images, and all their hilltop shrines.
\v 53 Take the land from there and start to live there, because I’ve given it to all of you to own.
\v 54 Use lots to divide the land up for your clans. The larger groups must get more than the smaller groups, then whatever the lot gives, that will be the area to be given within the land for that ancestral tribe.
\p
\v 55 If you don’t drive out the current inhabitants, then they’ll become like barbs in your eyes and thorns in your sides, and they’ll harass you all when you live there.
\v 56 If that happens, then I’ll give you all the punishment that I intended for them.”
\c 34
\s1 The boundaries within Canaan
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the people that you’re all about to enter Canaan—the land that will become your inheritance. The boundaries will be:
\p
\v 3 “The \bd southern\bd* border will extend from the Zin wilderness along the Edom border across to the Dead sea on the east,
\v 4 cross south of the Scorpion Pass, continue to Zin, and go south of Kadesh-Barnea. Then it will go on to Hatsar-Adar and proceed to Atsmon,
\v 5 From Atsmon \add it will extend west\add* to the riverbed at the border with Egypt, and then across to the \add Mediterranean\add* sea.
\p
\v 6 “The \bd western\bd* border will be the Mediterranean sea.
\p
\v 7 “The \bd northern\bd* border will start at the Mediterranean sea and extend west to Mt. Hor,
\v 8 and then on to Levo-Hamat and onward to Tsedad.
\v 9 \add From there\add* the border will extend to Zifron, and it will end at Hatsar-Eynan, completing the northern border.
\p
\v 10 “The \bd eastern\bd* border will begin at Hatsar-Eynan and extend \add south\add* to Shefam,
\v 11 then go downhill to Rivlah east of Ayin, then along the hills to the east of Kineret lake.
\v 12 Then the border will extend \add south\add* along the Jordan \add river\add* and end at the Dead sea.
\p “Those will be your country’s borders.”
\p
\v 13 Then Moses continued, “That will be the land that \add must be distributed\add* by lot. It’ll be given by Yahweh as an inheritance for the nine tribes and the Manasseh half-tribe
\v 14 \add as\add* the Reuben and Gad tribes and the Manasseh half-tribe have already received their inheritance.
\v 15 \add Yes,\add* the \add other\add* two and a half tribes have taken up their inheritance on the eastern side of the Jordan \add river\add* across from Jericho.
\s1 The teachers egpepamaaren of land
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 17 “These are the men who will distribute the land: the priest Eleazar and Nun’s son Joshua,
\v 18 as well as one leader from each tribe to allocate the land that will be their inheritance.
\v 19 Those \add tribe\add* leaders are:
\li1 From Judah, Yefuneh’s son Caleb,
\li1
\v 20 From Simeon, Amihud’s son Samuel,
\li1
\v 21 From Benjamin, Kislon’s son Elidad,
\li1
\v 22 From Dan, Yogli’s son Buki,
\li1
\v 23 From Manasseh, Efod’s son Haniyel,
\li1
\v 24 From Ephraim, Shiftan’s son Kemuel,
\li1
\v 25 From Zebulun, Parnak’s son Elitsafan,
\li1
\v 26 From Issachar, Azan’s son Paltiel,
\li1
\v 27 From Asher, Shelomi’s son Ahihud,
\li1
\v 28 From Naphtali, Amihud’s son Pedahel.”
\p
\v 29 The above are \add the men\add* that Yahweh put in charge of dividing up Canaan as the inheritance for the Israelites.
\c 35
\s1 Cities for the Levites
\p
\v 1 While they were there on the Moab plains beside the Jordan \add river\add*, Yahweh told Moses.
\v 2 “Tell the \add Israelites\add* that out of the land that they’ll own, they must give the Levites cities for them to live in, as well as some pastureland around those cities.
\v 3 The cities will be for them to live in, and that pastureland will be for their herds and their flocks, and for their other animals.
\v 4 The pastureland must extend out half a kilometre around the walls of the Levites cities.
\v 5 Then going out a kilometre in each compass direction, that will also be their land.\f + \fr 35:5 \ft It’s not exactly clear what different activities would occur in these two rings of land, but remember that as much food as possible and animals would have to be brought into the city as soon as the residents received news of an approaching army.\f*
\v 6 The Levites must be given forty-two cities to live in, plus you all must designate six refuge cities so that if someone accidentally kills another, they can flee to the refuge city.
\v 7 That would make a total of forty-eight cities, plus their pasturelands, to be given to the Levites.
\v 8 The larger tribes must give more cities than the smaller tribes, according to the area that they inherit.”
\s1 The refuge cities
\r (Deu. 19:1-13; Josh. 20:1-9)
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 10 “Tell the Israelites that when you cross the Jordan \add river\add* to enter Canaan,
\v 11 you all must choose some cities to become ‘refuge cities’ \add so that\add* if someone accidentally kills another person, they can flee there.
\v 12 The cities will be places to stay safe from being avenged \add by the relatives\add* until the killer can be formally tried.
\v 13 You all must allocate six refuge cities—
\v 14 three on the eastern side of the Jordan, and there on the western side in Canaan.
\v 15 Both Israelites and foreigners living in the country can flee to \add any of\add* those six cities—anyone who kills \add another\add* person by accident.
\p
\v 16 “However, if someone strikes another person with a metal object and they die, then that person is a murderer, and murderers must be executed.
\v 17 Similarly, anyone who strikes another person when holding a \add rock or\add* stone, then that person is a murderer, and murderers must be executed.
\v 18 Again, if someone strikes another person with a piece of wood, then that person is a murderer, and murderers must be executed.
\v 19 The \add relative who\add* is avenging the \add spilt\add* blood must execute the murderer as soon as he finds them.
\p
\v 20 “If someone pushes another person in hatred, or lies in wait and then throws something at them,
\v 21 or angrily strikes them with their hand, and then they die, it must be regarded as murder, and when the avenger of their blood finds the murderer, he must execute them.
\p
\v 22 “But if someone pushes another person without any hateful \add intent\add*, or throws something at them without lying in wait,
\v 23 or drops a rock without noticing someone, and they die but they weren’t that person’s enemy and no harm was intended,
\v 24 then the community must judge between the one who killed and the avenger of the dead person, in accordance with these regulations.
\v 25 \add If the one who killed is declared innocent\add*, then the community must rescue them from the avenger and return them to the refuge city that they’d fled to, and that accidental killer must live there until after the death of the high priest (who had been anointed with sacred oil).
\v 26 However, if they then ever left the refuge city that they’d fled to,
\v 27 and the avenger found them outside the city boundary, then that relative of the dead person is permitted to kill that person and won’t be accountable for their blood.
\v 28 \add That’s\add* because the one who killed must remain in that city until the death of the high priest—they can only return to their family home after the high priest’s death.
\v 29 You all must \add follow\add* these regulations through the generations no matter where you live.
\p
\v 30 “\add If\add* someone murders \add another\add* person, they’re to be executed if there’s evidence from witnesses (\add plural,\add* not just one witness).
\v 31 That sentence can’t be changed to a fine and/or a payment to the deceased’s family—the murderer \em must\em* be executed.
\v 32 Similarly for the accidental killer who fled to a refuge city, they can’t elect to pay a fine and/or pay compensation in order to be able to return home before the high priest’s death.
\v 33 You mustn’t profane the land that you’re all going to \add by tolerating murder\add*, because \add human\add* blood pollutes the ground, and you all can’t restore the land with any sacrifice—only by the blood of the one who shed \add someone else’s\add* blood.
\v 34 You mustn’t profane the land that you’ll all be living in because I’ll also be living in it, as I will live among \add you\add* Israelites.”
\c 36
\s1 Women inheriting property
\p
\v 1 Now the leaders of the Gilead clans (Gilead was the son of Makir, son of Manasseh, one of Joseph’s sons) approached Moses and the Israelite leaders
\v 2 and said, “Yahweh commanded my master to distribute the land by lot as an inheritance for the people, and my master was \add also\add* commanded by Yahweh to give our brother Tselofehad’s inheritance to his daughters.
\v 3 However, if they \add ended up\add* being married by men from other tribes, then their inheritance would pass from our tribe to their husbands’ tribes, so we’d end up with less land as our tribal inheritance.
\v 4 Then when Israel celebrates the Jubilee \add Year\add*, then that inheritance will be added to the inheritance of their husbands’ tribes, and be deducted from the inheritance of our ancestral tribe.”
\p
\v 5 Then Moses gave a ruling to the Israelites in accordance with what Yahweh had told him, “These Yosefi men are correct.
\v 6 Yahweh’s command to Tselofehad’s daughters is that each of you may marry whoever you want as long as it’s someone from your own tribe.
\v 7 That way, land in Israel won’t move from tribe to tribe, because it will always stay as part of the inheritance given to that tribe.
\v 8 Any woman who inherits land can only marry someone from her own tribe, so that the people will \add always\add* retain ownership of their tribal inheritance.
\v 9 That inheritance mustn’t pass from tribe to tribe, but each Israelite tribe must keep hold of its inheritance.”
\p
\v 10 So Tselofehad’s daughters obeyed what Yahweh had told Moses,
\v 11 and the \add five\add* daughters (Mahlah, Tirtsah, Hoglah, Milkah, and Noah) married their cousins on their father’s side.
\v 12 The men they married were from the Manasseh tribe (Joseph’s son), so their land continued to belong to their father’s family and tribe.
\p
\v 13 All the above were the commands and regulations that Yahweh ordered Moses to tell the Israelites, \add while they were camped\add* on the Moab plains beside the Jordan \add river\add* opposite Jericho.
